window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-77481efb12a63f4e"]={"zh":"<blockquote>  <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。如需 Markdown 格式，请在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"managed-configuration\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">受管配置</h1> <p>受管配置允许企业管理员为 ChatGPT Work 和 Codex 设置受支持的强制要求与默认值。强制要求会限制用户和任务可以执行的操作；默认值则提供初始设置。具体支持情况取决于产品、客户端版本和执行环境。</p> <p>对于受支持的云端管理设置，请先在管理控制台的 <strong>Agent security</strong> 中进行配置。常见任务可以使用设置控件；也可以选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 查看、复制或编辑配置文本。两种视图编辑的是同一项策略；TOML 还会显示没有专用控件的受支持字段。使用云端管理设置不需要分发本地文件。身份验证限制和设备管理部署需要单独进行<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">本地设置</a>。</p> <p>企业管理员可以通过以下方式控制受支持的本地客户端行为：</p> <ul> <li><strong>强制要求</strong>：管理员强制执行、用户无法覆盖的限制。</li> <li><strong>配置默认值</strong>：由系统或云端管理的 <code>config.toml</code> 设置，用户仍可覆盖。</li> <li><strong>旧版受管默认值</strong>：受支持的客户端启动时应用的 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 初始值。用户可在一次运行期间更改设置；客户端下次启动时会重新应用这些默认值。</li> </ul> <p>有关 Global 基线、环境覆盖项以及协调器和执行器字段清单，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-a-policy-in-agent-security\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"agent-security\">在 Agent security 中配置策略</h2> <p>在启用了 Agent security 的工作区中，工作区所有者、工作区管理员以及拥有 Codex 管理权限的成员都可以管理策略。能够打开策略编辑器，并不代表成员也获准使用该策略所控制的功能。</p> <p>使用 <strong>Workspace default</strong> 作为工作区基线。特定用户、群组或角色需要不同设置时，使用 <strong>Overrides</strong>。受众覆盖项决定策略适用于谁；环境设置决定受支持的执行控制项适用于哪些环境。</p> <ol> <li>打开 <strong>Admin Console</strong> &gt; <strong>Agent security</strong>，然后选择 <strong>Workspace default</strong>，或在 <strong>Overrides</strong> 下选择要编辑的策略。</li> <li>打开 <strong>Requirements</strong> 设置强制限制，或打开 <strong>Defaults</strong> 设置成员可以在这些限制范围内更改的初始值。</li> <li>将审批、网页搜索和其他适用于整个智能体的设置保留在 <strong>Global</strong> 中。若有对应选项，请在相关设置卡片中使用 <strong>Add environment settings</strong>，为受支持的执行设置添加 <strong>Local Environments</strong> 或 <strong>Codex Cloud Environments</strong>。空的环境文档会继承 Global 设置；它不会复制或固定当前基线。</li> <li>使用本指南介绍的控件。对于高级字段，请选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，编辑相应范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 或 <code>config.toml</code>。选择 <strong>Use settings controls</strong> 可返回可视化编辑器。</li> <li>查看验证消息，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。在规则编辑器中应用规则只会更新草稿；它本身不会保存策略。</li> <li>按照<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=confirm-the-admin-and-employee-experience\">确认管理员和员工实际体验</a>中的说明，检查策略选择结果和受支持客户端的行为。</li> </ol> <p>环境编辑器及其支持的字段取决于工作区和策略。在将 Global 设置移入某个环境之前，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=where-settings-apply\">设置适用范围</a>。Global 中的设置不一定适用于每种产品或执行路径。</p> <p><a id=\"copy-or-reuse-a-configuration\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_2\">复制或复用配置</h3> <p>可视化编辑器并不取代 TOML。选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，即可复制配置、保留参考副本，或在其他策略中复用设置。选择 <strong>Use settings controls</strong> 可切回设置控件。</p> <ol> <li>打开来源策略并选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>。</li> <li>选择要复制的范围：<strong>Global</strong> 或一个可用的环境选项卡。根据复用需求，复制 <strong>Requirements</strong>（<code>requirements.toml</code>）、<strong>Defaults</strong>（<code>config.toml</code>）中的文本，或两者都复制。</li> <li>打开目标策略，选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，并选择对应范围。将强制要求粘贴到 <code>requirements.toml</code>，将默认值粘贴到 <code>config.toml</code>。替换文本前先检查目标策略中已有的设置；保留仍需生效的其他设置。</li> <li>查看验证消息，以及目标策略的适用对象和优先级，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。在依赖复制来的策略之前，先确认客户端实际行为符合预期。</li> </ol> <p>复制的文本包含该策略和范围中编写的设置；但不包含策略分配关系、策略顺序、其他环境文档，也不包含由其他策略和设备设置合并得出的有效配置。需要时，请单独复制环境设置。切换视图或复制文本都不会保存策略更改。</p> <p>试用下方示例：更改一个设置，再选择 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 查看对应配置。切回 <strong>Use settings controls</strong> 可继续尝试其他设置。</p> <blockquote> <p>插图说明：Agent security 设置及其 TOML 表示的交互示例。初始强制要求允许按需审批、只读沙盒并禁用网页搜索。更改示例设置并选择 Edit as TOML，即可查看对应字段；选择 Use settings controls 可返回相同的示例选项。此示例不会更改工作区策略。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"choose-the-right-control\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_3\">选择合适的控制项</h3> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>任务</th> <th>配置位置</th> <th>作用</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">限制浏览器和原生应用访问</a></td> <td><strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Desktop feature availability</strong>、<strong>Site rules</strong> 和 <strong>App rules</strong></td> <td>控制受支持的桌面功能是否可用，以及这些功能可以访问哪些网站或应用。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\">限制审批、沙盒或网页搜索选项</a></td> <td><strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Policy guardrails</strong></td> <td>限制受支持客户端可以选择的选项。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">设置审批、权限、搜索或工具的初始行为</a></td> <td><strong>Defaults</strong></td> <td>设置成员可以在强制要求范围内自行更改的初始值。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access\">限制命令的网络目的地</a></td> <td>要求 &gt; 网络使用控制（Requirements &gt; Network use controls）</td> <td>为受支持的命令流量应用受管网络规则。它不是适用于整个工作区的互联网开关。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-plugins-and-managed-hooks\">配置插件与受管 Hooks</a></td> <td><strong>Edit as TOML</strong></td> <td>配置没有专用可视化控件的受支持策略字段。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">在登录前限制登录方式或工作区</a></td> <td>本地系统强制要求或 macOS MDM</td> <td>在加载云端策略之前应用身份验证限制。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"control-browser-and-computer-use\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">控制浏览器与计算机使用</h2> <p>对于受支持的桌面客户端，请在管理控制台的 <strong>Agent security</strong> 中管理浏览器和原生应用的访问权限。先使用界面控件；下方的 TOML 示例可作为基于文件分发策略的配置参考，也适用于需要通过 TOML 编辑器设置的情形。</p> <p>Browser Use 控制智能体在浏览器中的操作；Computer Use 控制智能体在原生桌面应用中的操作。内置浏览器面板的设置与 Browser Use 分开管理。关于不同浏览器路径的区别，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">浏览器会话与现有登录状态</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"before-you-begin\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">开始之前</h3> <p>使用有权管理 Agent Security 策略的账户。确认策略面向哪些人员，以及需要支持哪些客户端版本和操作系统。请先阅读 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a>，了解策略分配、Global 设置和受支持的环境覆盖项。</p> <p>使用 <strong>Requirements</strong> 设置用户无法自行放宽的限制；<strong>Defaults</strong> 则用于设置这些限制范围内的初始值。允许访问某个网站或应用，并不等于安装插件、授予操作系统权限、为用户登录，或批准仍需审核的操作。</p> <p>在 <strong>Desktop feature availability</strong> 下，检查 <strong>Browser use</strong>、<strong>External browser use</strong> 和 <strong>Computer use</strong>。<strong>Force off</strong> 会禁用相应功能；网站或应用的允许规则无法覆盖此设置。还要单独检查 <strong>Full CDP access</strong>；<strong>Block user opt-in</strong> 会阻止用户自行启用该功能。</p> <p>这些桌面控件不会配置托管式 Work Cloud 浏览器。请在 <strong>Admin Console &gt; Permissions &amp; roles &gt; Workspace capabilities &gt; Cloud computer capabilities</strong> 下单独管理 <strong>Cloud browser use</strong> 和 <strong>Cloud network access</strong>。另请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\">Agent Security 对 Work Cloud 的适用方式</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-browser-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">配置浏览器访问</h3> <ol> <li>打开 <strong>Admin Console &gt; Agent security</strong>。使用 <strong>Workspace default</strong> 设置工作区基线，或在 <strong>Overrides</strong> 下打开相应策略。在 <strong>Requirements</strong> 中进入 <strong>Desktop browser controls</strong>。</li> <li>打开 <strong>Site rules</strong> 中 <strong>Default</strong> 行的操作菜单，然后选择 <strong>Edit Default</strong>。若要仅允许访问获批网站，请将 <strong>Site access</strong> 设为 <strong>Block</strong>，再选择 <strong>Apply rule</strong>。匹配的网站规则如果保留 <strong>Use default rule</strong>，就会采用 <strong>Default</strong> 行提供的值。</li> <li>选择 <strong>Add site rule</strong>。在 <strong>Site or pattern</strong> 中填写一个来源（origin）：包括协议、主机名和可选端口。例如，<code>https://example.com</code> 有效；<code>https://example.com/files</code> 无效。不要包含路径、查询参数或片段。HTTP 与 HTTPS 属于不同来源；如果端口不是该协议的默认端口，请明确填写。</li> <li>设置 <strong>Site access</strong>，并检查 <strong>Downloads</strong>、<strong>Uploads</strong> 和 <strong>Debug/CDP</strong>。根据需要选择 <strong>Allow</strong> 或 <strong>Block</strong>，也可以保留 <strong>Use default rule</strong>。<strong>Debug/CDP</strong> 控制浏览器调试所用的完整 Chrome DevTools Protocol 访问权限。选择 <strong>Allow</strong> 后，常规策略与审批检查仍会继续执行。</li> <li>检查该网站的 <strong>Auto-review</strong>、<strong>Always allow</strong> 和 <strong>Approval duration</strong>。<strong>Approval duration</strong> 提供 <strong>This turn</strong> 和 <strong>This thread</strong> 两种时长。还要在 <strong>Desktop browser controls</strong> 中检查 <strong>Browser history</strong>、<strong>Save site approvals</strong> 和 <strong>Auto-approve requests</strong>。</li> <li>选择 <strong>Add rule</strong>，检查验证提示，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。在会接收此策略的受支持客户端上，分别测试允许访问的网站和被阻止的网站；上传、下载及其他受限操作也要分开测试。</li> </ol> <blockquote> <p>插图说明：Agent Security 网站规则编辑器，展示网站访问、文件传输、调试和审批控件。</p> </blockquote> <p>关闭浏览器操作的自动审核后，这些操作会改为提交给用户审批；这不会关闭模型安全监控。</p> <p>多个来源模式同时匹配时，每项设置都采用限制最严格的值。本地配置无法放宽受管策略中的拒绝规则。受管网络拒绝规则和独占允许列表也适用于浏览器工具。来源模式及审批行为请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">浏览器策略字段</a>。</p> <p>Computer Use 也可以像操作原生应用一样操作浏览器。Browser Use 的网站规则和功能规则不适用于这种路径。请另外检查原生应用访问设置；另请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">浏览器安全边界</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-native-app-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">配置原生应用访问</h3> <ol> <li>在 <strong>Requirements</strong> 中进入 <strong>Desktop app controls &gt; App rules</strong>。</li> <li>使用 <strong>Default</strong> 行的操作菜单，为没有匹配规则的应用配置访问权限。若要将 Computer Use 限制为获批应用，请默认阻止访问，再为这些应用添加允许规则。</li> <li>选择 <strong>Add app rule</strong>。在 <strong>Edit rule</strong> 对话框中，从 <strong>App</strong> 选择 <strong>macOS bundle ID</strong>、<strong>Windows AUMID</strong> 或 <strong>Windows executable</strong>。对于 bundle ID 或 Application User Model ID (AUMID)，请填写准确的 <strong>App identifier</strong>。对于 <strong>Windows executable</strong>，请填写 <strong>Publisher name</strong> 和 <strong>Product name</strong>；<strong>Binary name</strong> 为可选项。应使用已核实的应用身份信息，而不是显示名称或文件路径。</li> <li>为该规则设置 <strong>App access</strong>，然后选择 <strong>Add rule</strong>。检查 <strong>Desktop app controls</strong> 下的 <strong>Save app approvals</strong>，阅读验证提示，最后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。关闭已保存的审批后，当前会话仍可进行审批；允许保存审批也不代表应用已经获批。</li> <li>在部署涉及的每种受支持操作系统上，分别测试获准访问的应用和被阻止的应用。确认操作系统权限仍然生效，且用户仍须提供所需审批。</li> </ol> <blockquote> <p>插图说明：Agent Security 原生应用规则编辑器，展示 macOS bundle ID、应用标识符和应用访问权限。</p> </blockquote> <p>关于 Windows 身份标识要求，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">Computer Use 策略字段</a>；关于受管 Mac 的<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-locked-computer-use\">锁定使用限制</a>，也请一并查看。</p> <p><a id=\"check-the-effective-policy\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_8\">检查实际生效的策略</h3> <p>如果允许访问的网站或应用仍被阻止，请检查其他匹配规则、策略分配与优先级、设备要求以及用户配置。某一来源中的允许规则不能绕过其他适用的拒绝规则。另请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">要求的适用位置与优先级</a>。</p> <p>如果某项设置没有生效，请确认客户端、操作系统和策略分发路径都支持它。尤其要留意带本地计算机访问能力的 Work Cloud：部分字段仅获部分支持，或需要进一步验证。另请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考中的 Work 兼容性</a>。</p> <p>如果用户无法使用某项功能或必需插件，请分别检查<a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">工作区权限</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">插件控件</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"toml-configuration-reference\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"toml\">TOML 配置参考</h3> <p>以下示例表达的是同一项策略意图，使用 <code>requirements.toml</code> 配置。管理文件或使用 TOML 编辑器时可参考这些示例。Agent Security 提供的控件无需通过编辑本地文件即可使用。</p> <p>下面的浏览器示例默认拒绝访问，但允许访问 <code>https://example.com</code>；同时阻止该网站上的上传和完整 CDP 访问，并将网站访问审批限制在当前轮次内：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[browser_use]\nallow_history_access = false\nallow_global_persistent_approval = false\n\n[browser_use.default_origin_policy]\naccess = \"deny\"\n\n[browser_use.origins.\"https://example.com\"]\naccess = \"allow\"\nuploads = \"deny\"\ndownloads = \"allow\"\nfull_cdp_access = \"deny\"\npersistent_approval = false\naccess_approval_lifetime = \"turn\"\n</code></pre> <p>若要关闭浏览器操作的自动审核，请将 <code>browser_use.disable_auto_review = true</code>；也可以在某个来源策略上设置 <code>auto_review = \"deny\"</code>，仅对该来源施加限制。</p> <p>若要为符合条件的 Enterprise 工作区启用<a href=\"#/article/a-d8e7ab2e8f18b498\">站点工具</a>，请设置 <code>browser_use.allow_webmcp = true</code>。此托管式选择加入项适用于桌面应用的内置浏览器。设为 <code>false</code> 或省略此项时，仍会执行常规功能可用性检查；<code>false</code> 并不强制阻止该功能。用户偏好和站点权限仍然适用。</p> <p>下面的原生应用示例允许在 macOS 上使用 Calculator，默认拒绝其他应用，并阻止应用审批在不同会话间保留：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[computer_use]\ndefault_app_access = \"deny\"\nallow_persistent_approval = false\n\n[computer_use.macos.bundle_ids]\n\"com.apple.calculator\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>Windows 策略可以使用 <code>computer_use.windows.aumids</code> 标识打包应用，或使用 <code>computer_use.windows.exes</code> 标识可执行文件。可执行文件规则必须包含 <code>publisher_name</code>、<code>product_name</code> 和 <code>access</code>；<code>binary_name</code> 为可选项。</p> <p>所有受支持的字段和值请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> 参考</a>。若要直接禁用某项功能，而不是逐个限制网站或应用，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=pin-feature-flags\">固定功能标志</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"other-desktop-controls\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_9\">其他桌面控件</h3> <p>以下桌面控件涉及功能可用性、Locked Use、Appshots 和设备远程控制。功能可用性与 Locked Use 使用专门设置；Appshots 和设备远程控制则通过 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 配置。有关桌面应用网络流量的目的地限制，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-desktop-app-network-destinations\">控制桌面应用网络目的地</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"pin-feature-flags\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_10\">固定功能开关</h4> <p><a id=\"disable-codex-feature-surfaces\"></a></p> <p>对于 Agent Security 中提供的桌面功能，请打开 <strong>Global &gt; Requirements &gt; Desktop feature availability</strong>。将 <strong>In-app browser</strong>、<strong>Browser use</strong>、<strong>External browser use</strong> 或 <strong>Computer use</strong> 设为 <strong>Force off</strong>，即可禁用相应功能，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。对于 <strong>Full CDP access</strong>，请选择 <strong>Block user opt-in</strong>，防止用户自行启用。允许某项功能，并不能覆盖其产品、平台或分阶段推出情况所施加的可用性限制。</p> <p>对于其他受支持的<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=feature-flags\">功能标志</a>，请使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，并编辑 Global 范围内的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 文档或受支持的本地要求来源。以下示例同时包含运行时和桌面专用的功能固定设置：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[features]\nhooks = true\nunified_exec = false\n\n# Disable surface-specific features when needed.\nbrowser_use = false\nbrowser_use_full_cdp_access = false\nbrowser_use_external = false\nin_app_browser = false\nin_app_updates = false\ncomputer_use = false\n</code></pre> <p>运行时功能请使用 <code>config.toml</code> 的 <code>[features]</code> 表中规定的标准键。本地运行环境会调整其识别出的功能，使其符合这些固定值，并拒绝与之冲突的 <code>config.toml</code> 写入或配置方案文件中的功能设置。</p> <p>受管设置中若将 <code>unified_exec = false</code>，命令仍可执行，但可恢复进程和 <code>write_stdin</code> 工具会被禁用。用户配置无法关闭这项功能。</p> <ul> <li><code>in_app_browser = false</code> 会禁用内置浏览器面板。</li> <li>在支持此功能的地方，重启后 <code>in_app_updates = false</code> 会禁用 ChatGPT 桌面应用自身的更新程序。它不会影响外部软件包部署，也不会延长较旧应用版本的支持期限。设置和部署说明请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-24c62848a3326493\">管理应用更新</a>。</li> <li><code>browser_use = false</code> 会禁用受支持的 Browser Use 功能。External Browser Use 与原生应用 Computer Use 使用单独的控件。</li> <li><code>browser_use_full_cdp_access = false</code> 会禁用本地运行环境中的完整 CDP 访问，包括 Browser Developer 模式，并阻止 ChatGPT 桌面应用开启相应设置。</li> <li><code>browser_use_external = false</code> 会禁用通过受支持的浏览器扩展执行智能体驱动的 Browser Use。</li> <li><code>computer_use = false</code> 会禁用原生应用 Computer Use，包括将浏览器作为原生应用操作；同时也会禁用 Record &amp; Replay 以及相关安装或设置流程。</li> </ul> <p><strong>仅禁用 Browser Use 并不能阻止所有浏览器操作路径。</strong>只要原生应用 Computer Use 和应用访问权限仍获允许，它依然可以操作浏览器；Browser Use 的网站规则与功能规则不适用于这条路径。若要阻止这种操作，请将 <strong>Computer use</strong> 设为 <strong>Force off</strong>（<code>computer_use = false</code>）；如果还要允许其他原生应用，则可在 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-native-app-access\">App rules</a> 中拒绝浏览器应用。另请分别检查 <strong>External browser use</strong> 与 <strong>In-app browser</strong>。关于各路径适用的控件，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">浏览器会话与现有登录状态</a>。</p> <p>如果省略这些键，策略就不会禁止这些功能；它们仍受客户端、平台和逐步推出情况等常规可用性条件限制。</p> <p><a id=\"restrict-locked-computer-use\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_11\">限制锁定使用模式</h4> <p>若要阻止受管 Mac 用户启用 <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c?anchor=locked-use\">Locked Use</a>，请打开 <strong>Global &gt; Requirements &gt; Desktop app controls</strong>，将 <strong>Allow use while locked</strong> 设为 <strong>Disabled</strong>，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。如果某台 Mac 已启用 Locked Use，此设置不会将其关闭。</p> <p>在 TOML 编辑器或设备管理分发中，可使用等效的要求设置：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[computer_use]\nallow_locked_computer_use = false\n</code></pre> <p>此要求会移除启用锁定使用模式的控件，但不会关闭已经启用的锁定使用模式。如果省略此项，仍由产品的正常可用性检查和用户本地设置决定。</p> <p><a id=\"disable-appshots\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"appshots\">禁用 Appshots</h4> <p>Appshots 允许受支持的客户端附带应用快照。若要对云端管理的用户禁用此功能，请在 Agent Security 中打开 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，并在 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中设置顶层字段 <code>allow_appshots</code>。设备管理部署则应使用适用的本地要求来源：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_appshots = false\n</code></pre> <p>在支持 Appshots 的地方，<code>allow_appshots = false</code> 会将其禁用。如果省略此键，强制要求不会限制 Appshots，仍按产品正常的可用性检查处理。通过 <code>configRequirements/read</code> 读取有效要求的 app-server 客户端也会收到相同的 <code>allowAppshots</code> 限制；省略 <code>allowAppshots</code> 或将其设为 <code>null</code>，都不会禁用 Appshots。</p> <p><a id=\"disable-device-remote-control\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_12\">禁用设备远程控制</h4> <p>若要对云端管理的用户禁用<a href=\"#/article/a-171fdaa74f75310f?anchor=pick-up-work-from-another-device\">设备远程控制</a>，请在 Agent Security 中打开 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，并在 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中设置顶层字段 <code>allow_remote_control</code>。也可以在受支持的本地要求来源中设置 <code>allow_remote_control</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_remote_control = false\n</code></pre> <p>在支持设备远程控制的地方，<code>allow_remote_control = false</code> 会将其禁用。如果省略此键，强制要求不会限制设备远程控制，仍按产品正常的可用性检查处理。此要求不会禁用 SSH 远程连接。</p> <p><a id=\"set-approvals-and-execution-permissions\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_13\">设置审批与执行权限</h2> <p>本节介绍如何设置审批行为、权限边界和初始默认值。请先阅读<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\">策略护栏</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">默认值</a>。后续任务将介绍审核者强制要求、自定义权限配置文件、文件系统限制、主机覆盖项和命令规则对应的 TOML 要求设置。</p> <p><a id=\"admin-enforced-requirements-requirementstoml\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"requirementstoml\">管理员强制要求（requirements.toml）</h3> <p>强制要求用于限制与安全相关的设置，例如审批策略、审批审查者、自动审查策略、沙盒模式、权限配置方案、网页搜索模式、受管 Hooks、用户可启用的 MCP 服务器，以及可使用的插件市场来源。解析配置时（例如读取 <code>config.toml</code>、<a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=profiles\">配置方案文件</a>或 CLI 配置覆盖项），如果某个值与强制规则冲突，本地客户端会回退到兼容的值并通知用户。如果你配置了 <code>mcp_servers</code> 允许列表，客户端只有在 MCP 服务器的名称和身份都与获批条目匹配时才会启用它；否则会将其禁用。</p> <p>强制要求还可以通过 <code>[features]</code> 表限制<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=feature-flags\">功能开关</a>。功能并非总与安全直接相关，但企业可按需固定其取值。未列出的键不受限制；相关配置表为 <code>requirements.toml</code>。</p> <p>Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本建议使用<a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">权限配置方案</a>，并搭配 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> 和受管的 <code>default_permissions</code>。只有仍在使用旧版 <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code> 配置的部署，才应使用 <code>sandbox_mode</code>。</p> <p>完整键列表请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">配置参考中的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 一节</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_14\">设置审批、沙盒和网页搜索护栏</h3> <p>使用 <strong>Requirements &gt; Policy guardrails</strong> 限制受支持客户端可选择的选项。这些控件用于约束配置，但不会选择初始值；初始值需另行在 <strong>Defaults</strong> 下设置。</p> <ol> <li>在 <strong>Global</strong> 中打开 <strong>Approval policies</strong>，选择允许使用的审批方式。例如，<strong>Ask when needed</strong> 允许智能体针对沙盒权限提升等操作请求审批。<strong>Never ask</strong> 会阻止发出审批请求，但并不授予不受限制的执行权限。</li> <li>打开 <strong>Sandbox modes</strong>，限制可选择的旧版沙盒模式。<strong>Read-only</strong> 会限制写入；<strong>Workspace write</strong> 允许在工作区和已配置的可写根目录中写入；<strong>Full access</strong> 则会移除文件系统沙盒限制。若部署使用权限配置文件而非 <code>sandbox_mode</code>，请改用<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">权限配置文件要求</a>。</li> <li>在 <strong>Global</strong> 中打开 <strong>Web search modes</strong>，限制网页搜索。选择 <strong>Cached</strong> 可允许使用缓存搜索、但不允许实时搜索；选择 <strong>Disabled</strong> 可关闭网页搜索。网页搜索限制与命令网络规则彼此独立。</li> <li>检查允许的选项及对应默认值，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。</li> </ol> <p>如果某项要求未设置，则该策略不会施加相应限制；但其他适用策略仍然生效。<strong>Ask for untrusted commands</strong> 选项对应要求允许列表中的 <code>untrusted</code>，它不会恢复已停用的显式设置 <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code>。如果遇到这种情况，请按下方迁移指南操作。</p> <p>若要限制网页搜索，请使用 <strong>Global &gt; Requirements &gt; Policy guardrails &gt; Web search modes</strong>。对应的 TOML 设置为：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\"] # \"disabled\" remains implicitly allowed\n</code></pre> <p><code>allowed_web_search_modes = []</code> 表示只允许 <code>\"disabled\"</code>。例如，<code>allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\"]</code> 会阻止实时网页搜索，即使会话使用 <code>danger-full-access</code> 也一样。</p> <p><a id=\"set-configuration-defaults\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_15\">设置配置默认值</h3> <p>默认值为成员提供一套初始配置。它们既不会强制执行限制，也不会授予工作区功能的访问权限。</p> <ol> <li>打开该策略的 <strong>Defaults</strong> 卡片。将适用于整个智能体的默认值保留在 <strong>Global</strong> 中；受支持的执行默认值则放在可用的环境选项卡中。</li> <li>在 <strong>Command execution</strong> 下，选择初始的 <strong>Approval policy</strong> 和 <strong>Approval reviewer</strong>，以及适用的 <strong>Sandbox mode</strong> 或 <strong>Default permissions</strong>。审核者决定默认由谁审核审批请求；审批策略则决定何时可以发起请求。</li> <li>在 <strong>Web search</strong> 下，选择初始的 <strong>Web search</strong> 模式和 <strong>Web search context</strong>。在 <strong>Apps and tools</strong> 下，检查受支持应用工具对应的 <strong>Apps/connectors</strong>、<strong>Open-world tools</strong> 和 <strong>Destructive tools</strong>。</li> <li>确认这些默认值符合你的 <strong>Requirements</strong>，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。</li> </ol> <p>对于没有可视化控件的受支持默认设置（例如插件市场配置），请使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>。这些云端默认值遵循<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">常规配置优先级</a>，不同于<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=precedence-and-layering\">旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 的优先级</a>；在启动时，旧版方式会让受管默认值优先于本地配置。</p> <p><a id=\"example-requirementstoml\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"requirementstoml_1\">requirements.toml 示例</h3> <p>下面的示例会禁止使用 <code>--ask-for-approval never</code> 和 <code>--sandbox danger-full-access</code>（包括 <code>--yolo</code>）：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"untrusted\", \"on-request\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <p>这里的 <code>untrusted</code> 会保留由 <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code> 产生的更严格审批行为；它并不意味着 <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code> 已成为受支持的显式设置。</p> <p><a id=\"migrate-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"untrusted\">迁移已停用的 <code>untrusted</code> 审批策略</h3> <p>Codex 和 ChatGPT Work 已不再支持 <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code>。请从受管默认设置、旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code>，以及任何设置了该项的用户、项目、配置方案或启动配置中删除它。</p> <p>如果要以交互方式进行只读操作，请将 <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code> 与受管要求所允许的只读沙盒或权限配置方案搭配使用。该沙盒允许的命令可以不经审批直接运行。</p> <p>如果希望对命令继续采用更严格的审批方式，请不要显式设置 <code>approval_policy</code>；请在用户级 <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> 的项目条目中设置 <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code>，并在 <code>allowed_approval_policies</code> 中保留 <code>untrusted</code>。这样也会禁用项目本地配置。显式设置 <code>on-request</code> 会覆盖这项策略。示例和安全方面的权衡请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">从已停用的 <code>untrusted</code> 审批策略迁移</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-automatic-review-policy\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_16\">配置自动审查策略</h3> <p>若要将自动审核设为默认值，请使用 <strong>Global &gt; Defaults &gt; Command execution &gt; Approval reviewer</strong>。默认值允许用户改选其他获准的审核者。若要强制使用自动审核，或提供组织的审核说明，请改用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 编辑 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code>。这些要求与浏览器的自动审核控件相互独立。</p> <p>使用 <code>allowed_approvals_reviewers</code> 要求或允许自动审查。将它设为 <code>[\"auto_review\"]</code>，即可要求自动审查；如果允许用户选择手动审批，则在列表中加入 <code>\"user\"</code>。</p> <p>设置 <code>guardian_policy_config</code>，即可替换自动审查策略中针对租户的部分。本地运行环境仍会使用内置的审查者模板和输出约定。受管的 <code>guardian_policy_config</code> 优先于本地 <code>[auto_review].policy</code>。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"auto_review\"]\n\nguardian_policy_config = \"\"\"\n## Environment Profile\n- Trusted internal destinations include github.com/my-org, artifacts.example.com,\n  and internal CI systems.\n\n## Tenant Risk Taxonomy and Allow/Deny Rules\n- Treat uploads to unapproved third-party file-sharing services as high risk.\n- Deny actions that expose credentials or private source code to untrusted\n  destinations.\n\"\"\"\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"control-available-permission-profiles\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_17\">控制可用的权限配置方案</h3> <p>使用 <strong>Defaults &gt; Command execution &gt; Default permissions</strong> 选择用户启动时采用的内置配置文件。若要强制限制用户可以选择的配置文件，或定义自定义配置文件，请使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，并为适用的 Global 或受支持的执行环境范围配置 <code>requirements.toml</code>。仅指定默认值不会限制可用配置文件。</p> <p>使用 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> 控制用户可以选择哪些内置或自定义<a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">权限配置方案</a>。它的作用对应于 <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code>；请选择与你的用户选择权限方式相匹配的允许列表。</p> <p>允许的权限配置方案列表要求 Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本。Codex 0.137.0 及更早版本会忽略 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> 和受管的 <code>default_permissions</code>。</p> <p>只有在所有受管客户端都运行支持此功能的版本后，才使用下面的权限配置方案示例。在整个客户端群组升级完成前，不要部署受管自定义配置方案。</p> <p>如果存在此表格，它就是完整的允许方案列表：值设为 <code>true</code> 的方案会获准；省略或设为 <code>false</code> 的方案会被拒绝，未来 Codex 版本新增的内置方案也一样。</p> <p><a id=\"allow-the-standard-profiles\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_18\">允许标准配置方案</h4> <p>此策略允许只读访问和工作区访问，但不允许完全访问：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n# \":danger-full-access\" is omitted, so it is denied.\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"add-a-managed-least-privilege-default\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_19\">添加受管的最小权限默认方案</h4> <p>管理员可以在同一个要求来源中定义自定义方案。请使用组织专属的方案名称，避免与用户已加载配置中的名称冲突。自定义名称不能以 <code>:</code> 开头，也不能使用保留名称 <code>filesystem</code>。</p> <p>不要将受管自定义方案部署给运行 Codex 0.137.0 或更早版本的客户端。这些客户端能识别方案表格，但不认识用于选择该方案的受管默认值。</p> <p>例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"acme_review_only\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\nacme_review_only = true\n# \":danger-full-access\" is intentionally omitted, so it is denied.\n\n[permissions.acme_review_only]\ndescription = \"Review code without modifying the workspace.\"\nextends = \":read-only\"\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"allow-only-enterprise-defined-profiles\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_20\">仅允许企业定义的配置方案</h4> <p>如果希望用户只能选择管理员定义的方案，请省略所有内置方案：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"acme_workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\nacme_workspace = true\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace]\ndescription = \"Workspace access with sensitive files denied.\"\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace.filesystem]\nglob_scan_max_depth = 3\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>即使用户不能直接选择内置的 <code>:workspace</code> 方案，自定义方案仍可以继承 <code>:workspace</code>。</p> <p><a id=\"turn-off-a-profile-allowed-by-another-source\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_21\">关闭由其他来源允许的配置方案</h4> <p>权限允许列表会按方案名称合并。由于云端要求的优先级高于系统要求，因此云端要求可以将某个方案设为 <code>false</code>，从而关闭系统文件中已允许的方案。</p> <p>云端要求：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \":read-only\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = false\n</code></pre> <p>系统要求：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true  # Not honored because cloud requirements set this to false.\n</code></pre> <p>请明确将 <code>default_permissions</code> 设为某个获准方案。若省略此项，只有在 <code>:workspace</code> 和 <code>:read-only</code> 都被明确允许时，本地运行环境才会默认使用 <code>:workspace</code>。如果没有 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code>，受管要求就不会限制用户可选的方案名称。每个条目都必须指向内置方案，或已在加载的配置文件、要求来源中定义的自定义方案。若要集中控制自定义方案的行为，请在受管要求中定义它们。</p> <p><a id=\"enforce-deny-read-requirements\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_22\">强制执行禁止读取要求</h3> <p>使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，将文件系统限制添加到适用的 Global 或受支持的执行环境 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中。这些限制会阻止读取敏感路径；仅设置只读默认值并不会隐藏文件。</p> <p>管理员可以通过 <code>[permissions.filesystem]</code>，按精确路径或 glob 模式禁止读取文件。用户无法通过本地配置放宽这些要求。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\n  # values can be absolute paths...\n  \"/**/*.env\",\n  # ...or relative to $HOME/%USERPROFILE% using `~`.\n  \"~/.ssh\",\n  # But relative paths starting with `./` are not allowed.\n]\n</code></pre> <p>如果配置了禁止读取的要求，本地运行环境会拒绝完全访问权限，并让本地执行保持在只读或工作区沙盒中，以便落实这些限制。在原生 Windows 上，受管 <code>deny_read</code> 适用于直接文件工具；Shell 子进程的读取不受这条沙盒规则约束。</p> <p><a id=\"override-sandbox-requirements-by-host\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_23\">按主机覆盖沙盒要求</h3> <p>在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 编写针对特定主机的要求。环境控件用于选择执行环境；主机模式则用于在受支持的运行时中匹配具体计算机。</p> <p>当同一项受管策略需要对不同主机应用不同沙盒要求时，请使用 <code>[[remote_sandbox_config]]</code>。例如，可以为笔记本电脑设置更严格的默认值，同时允许匹配的开发机或 CI 运行器写入工作区。目前，主机专属条目只会覆盖 <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\"]\n\n[[remote_sandbox_config]]\nhostname_patterns = [\"*.devbox.example.com\", \"runner-??.ci.example.com\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <p>本地运行环境会将每个 <code>hostname_patterns</code> 条目与尽可能可靠地解析出的主机名进行比较。如果可用，会优先采用完整限定域名；否则回退到本地主机名。比较时不区分大小写；<code>*</code> 可匹配任意长度的字符序列，<code>?</code> 可匹配单个字符。</p> <p>同一要求来源中的 <code>[[remote_sandbox_config]]</code> 条目按顺序检查，第一个匹配项生效。如果没有匹配项，本地运行环境会保留顶层的 <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code>。主机名匹配只用于选择策略，不能视为经过身份验证的设备证明。</p> <p><a id=\"enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"requirements\">从 requirements 强制执行命令规则</h3> <p>在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，将命令规则和 MCP 服务器限制添加到 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code>。命令规则会针对匹配的命令指定批准或拒绝决定；MCP 限制则会限制客户端可启用的服务器身份。<strong>Approval policies</strong> 控件不会编写这两类规则。</p> <p>管理员也可以使用 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中的 <code>[rules]</code> 表强制执行限制性命令规则。这些规则会与常规 <code>.rules</code> 文件合并；最终始终采用限制最严格的决定。</p> <p>与 <code>.rules</code> 不同，requirements 规则必须指定 <code>decision</code>，且取值只能是 <code>\"prompt\"</code> 或 <code>\"forbidden\"</code>（不能是 <code>\"allow\"</code>）。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"rm\" }], decision = \"forbidden\", justification = \"Use git clean -fd instead.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before mutating history.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>如果要限制本地客户端可启用的 MCP 服务器，请添加 <code>mcp_servers</code> 获批列表。对于 stdio 服务器，请按 <code>command</code> 匹配；对于可流式传输的 HTTP 服务器，请按 <code>url</code> 匹配：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.docs]\nidentity = { command = \"codex-mcp\" }\n\n[mcp_servers.remote]\nidentity = { url = \"https://example.com/mcp\" }\n</code></pre> <p><code>identity.command</code> 的字符串形式只会匹配所配置的 <code>command</code>，不会检查 <code>args</code>、<code>cwd</code>、<code>env</code> 或 <code>env_vars</code>。</p> <p>如果要限定完整的 stdio 调用，请同时匹配可执行文件和每个位置参数：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal.identity]\ncommand = { executable = \"/usr/local/bin/codex-mcp\", args = [\n  { match = \"exact\", value = \"serve\" },\n  { match = \"prefix\", value = \"--workspace=\" },\n] }\n</code></pre> <p>可执行文件、参数数量和参数顺序都必须一致。参数和 URL 规则支持 <code>exact</code>、<code>prefix</code> 和对完整值进行 <code>regex</code> 匹配。结构化命令规则仍不会检查 <code>cwd</code>、<code>env</code> 或 <code>env_vars</code>。插件自带的 MCP 服务器也使用相同的身份格式，配置位置为 <code>plugins.&lt;plugin&gt;.mcp_servers.&lt;server&gt;</code>。</p> <p>如果存在 <code>mcp_servers</code> 但它为空，本地客户端会禁用所有 MCP 服务器。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-network-access\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_24\">配置网络访问</h2> <p>命令联网与桌面应用联网使用不同的控件。先使用 <strong>Network use controls</strong> 管理受支持的命令流量，检查环境和运行时限制，再按需配置单独的桌面应用要求。浏览器网站规则仍位于<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">控制浏览器与 Computer Use</a>一节。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-network-access-requirements\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_25\">配置网络访问要求</h3> <p><code>[experimental_network]</code> 仍处于实验阶段，之后可能会变更。请先在用户实际使用的本地客户端版本和操作系统上验证这些要求，再考虑在整个企业范围内启用。Windows 支持仍有限；除非已在自己的环境中测试，否则不要将此策略应用于 Windows 用户。</p> <p>在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Requirements &gt; Network use controls</strong> 管理受支持的命令联网。可在 <strong>Global</strong> 中配置共享基线，也可在可用的 <strong>Local Environments</strong> 或 <strong>Codex Cloud Environments</strong> 选项卡中设置特定环境的执行规则。</p> <ol> <li>将 <strong>Managed networking</strong> 设为 <strong>On</strong>，以便对受管命令流量应用域名规则。仅添加域名规则并不会启用受管代理。</li> <li>在 <strong>Network rules</strong> 下选择 <strong>Add network rule</strong>。填写 <strong>Domain</strong>，例如 <code>api.openai.com</code> 或 <code>**.example.com</code>；不要填写 URL 协议或路径。在 <strong>Access</strong> 下选择 <strong>Allow</strong> 或 <strong>Deny</strong>，再选择 <strong>Save rule</strong> 更新草稿。其他目的地可重复此操作。</li> <li>如果必须阻止用户设置或逐域名审批扩大受管允许列表，请将 <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> 设为 <strong>On</strong>。请加入所需的 Allow 条目；只有 Deny 条目的列表并不会因此允许其他目的地。</li> <li>对于需要本地侦听端口或私有目的地的工作流，请检查 <strong>Allow local network access</strong>。直接连接本地网络可能绕过代理的域名规则。更改此设置前，请先阅读下方的 Codex Cloud 连接限制。</li> <li>检查继承的规则和验证提示，然后选择 <strong>Save changes</strong>。在每个受影响的执行环境中，分别测试应当成功的请求和应当被阻止的请求。</li> </ol> <p>将 <strong>Managed networking</strong> 设为 <strong>Off</strong>，会清除该范围中编写的网络规则，并保存明确的关闭值；这并不会禁用互联网访问。设为 <strong>Not set</strong> 则会移除该范围中编写的网络设置，使适用的策略或运行时默认值得以生效。移除环境设置后，Global 规则可能重新显现。高级网络字段，或可视化编辑器无法表达的旧版域名列表，请使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>。</p> <p>如果管理员需要集中定义网络访问要求，可在 <code>[experimental_network]</code> 中使用 <code>requirements.toml</code>。这些要求与用户的 <code>features.network_proxy</code> 开关彼此独立：即使没有启用该功能标记，也可以配置沙盒网络；但如果当前沙盒仍关闭网络，它不会因此授予命令网络访问权限。必须设置 <code>experimental_network.enabled = true</code> 才会启用受管代理；仅设置域名规则不会启动代理。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"**.example.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"blocked.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n\"**.exfil.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>只有在 <code>[experimental_network.domains]</code> 中也定义了由管理员管理的 <code>\"allow\"</code> 条目，并且希望这些规则成为唯一允许规则时，才设置 <code>experimental_network.managed_allowed_domains_only = true</code>。如果设为 <code>true</code>，但没有受管的允许规则，用户自行添加的域名允许规则就不会继续生效。不要同时使用标准的 <code>domains</code> 映射和旧版 <code>allowed_domains</code> 或 <code>denied_domains</code> 列表。</p> <p><code>*.example.com</code> 只匹配子域名；<code>**.example.com</code> 会匹配主域名及其子域名。若允许规则和拒绝规则同时匹配，以拒绝规则为准。</p> <p>域名语法、本机或专用网络目的地规则、拒绝优先于允许的行为，以及 DNS 重绑定限制，与<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=network-isolation\">代理审批和安全</a>中说明的沙盒网络行为相同。</p> <p>代理会转发沙盒内本地命令的网络流量。浏览器工具在访问源站前，也会检查受管网络拒绝规则和独占允许列表；这是另一项策略检查，不代表浏览器流量经过命令代理。该代理不会过滤网页搜索、应用和连接器、MCP 服务器、原生应用流量、Codex 服务请求或其他按功能分别管理的流量。请使用各功能对应的控制项：</p> <ul> <li>使用 <code>allowed_web_search_modes</code> 限制网页搜索。</li> <li>在受支持的客户端中，使用 <code>features.apps = false</code> 禁用应用和连接器集成，使用 <code>features.plugins = false</code> 禁用插件。</li> <li>使用受管的 <code>mcp_servers</code> 获批列表限制 MCP 服务器。</li> <li>使用 <code>browser_use</code>、<code>in_app_browser</code> 和 <code>computer_use</code> 等功能要求，限制浏览器及计算机使用能力。</li> <li>对于受支持的受管 Codex Cloud 命令，分别配置 Agent Security 强制要求和 Cloud 环境互联网设置。</li> </ul> <p>命令的域名允许列表不能取代上述针对各项功能的控制措施。</p> <p>在受支持的受管 Codex Cloud 执行路径中，Agent Security 要求会限制命令网络访问；Codex Cloud 环境的互联网设置则单独生效。Agent Security 中允许的域名不能覆盖 Cloud 环境互联网设置中的限制。这些命令网络控制本身并不会禁用托管网页搜索、应用或 MCP。ChatGPT Work Cloud 有单独的功能权限，不会继承这些 Agent Security 要求。</p> <p>受管命令允许列表适用于使用受管代理的命令。如果策略允许完整沙盒提升权限且相关操作已获批准，执行时可能绕过命令代理。范围狭窄的网络授权不同于完整沙盒提升权限。请为预期的安全边界配置强制审批和沙盒要求，并同时测试普通命令与提升权限后的命令。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>没有实际生效的允许目的地：</strong>当 <strong>Managed networking</strong> 与 <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> 均为 On 时，普通受管命令必须拥有实际生效的允许目的地。如果没有配置或继承任何 Allow 条目，这些命令就没有可访问的目的地。仅包含 Deny 条目的策略不会默认允许互联网中的其他地址。保存前请添加必需的 Allow 条目并检查继承规则。此限制适用于受管命令代理，并不适用于所有工具或经批准的完整沙盒权限提升。</p> <p><strong>Codex Cloud 的本地/私有网络连接：</strong> 即使目标域名已获允许，本地/私有网络连接的显式 Off 值仍可能阻止 Codex Cloud 访问上游代理。请检查最终生效的 <code>allow_local_binding</code> 值，并查明是哪项策略或设置提供了该值。在受支持的 Cloud 代理路径中，只有在没有适用要求、已选网络配置文件或代理功能设置提供该值时，默认值才为 true。继承而来的 false 仍视为显式设置。在支持的情况下，可以设置优先级更高的 Cloud 覆盖项，为 Codex Cloud 更改此值，而不改变 Local 使用的 Global 值。这不会添加域名 Allow 条目。依赖该覆盖项之前请核实执行器支持情况。不要把 Cloud 默认值套用到 Local。</p> </blockquote> <p>当环境要求为空时，适用 Global 要求。<strong>Managed networking</strong> 设为 <strong>Off</strong> 与 Cloud 环境的 Internet access 设置是两回事。有关执行范围的详情，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=execution-controls-executor\">Agent Security 执行控件</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"network-policy-precedence-and-runtime-limits\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_26\">网络策略优先级和运行时限制</h3> <p>同一策略中，优先顺序从高到低为：按操作系统区分的环境覆盖项 → 适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖项 → Global。跨策略时，高优先级策略仍优先，即使低优先级策略更具体也一样。部分强制要求有字段专属的合并规则。最终解析出的要求会由运行时强制执行。请把下面字段专属的网络行为与策略优先级分别考虑。</p> <p>以下示例比较同一策略中 Global 和环境设置的交互方式；前提是受管网络已配置，并且没有更高优先级的策略改变这些值。示例说明字段专属的合并与运行时行为。</p> <p><a id=\"set-different-domain-access-by-environment\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_27\">按环境设置不同的域名访问权限</h4> <p>对于同一策略中的同一域名规则，管理员可以在环境覆盖项中允许 Global 已拒绝的域名，也可以拒绝 Global 已允许的域名。没有环境覆盖项时，会继承 Global 规则。其他生效的 Deny 规则或访问控制仍可能阻止请求。</p> <p><strong>域名规则如何组合</strong></p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Global 规则</th> <th>环境规则</th> <th>域名策略结果</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Deny</td> <td>Allow</td> <td>环境覆盖项允许访问，但仍受下方条件限制。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow</td> <td>Deny</td> <td>在该环境中阻止访问。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow</td> <td>Allow</td> <td>这些域名规则允许访问。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Deny</td> <td>Deny</td> <td>这些域名规则阻止访问。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow 或 Deny</td> <td>无覆盖项</td> <td>继承 Global 规则。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>上述结果只比较同一策略中的同一个域名键，且不考虑改变结果的更高优先级策略。更高优先级的值会替换同一键，但其他继承的键仍然保留。某条 Allow 规则不能绕过另一条匹配的 Deny 规则，例如继承的通配符规则。空的环境映射不会清除继承的规则。</p> <p><strong>示例：</strong> 在 Global 中拒绝 <code>packages.example.com</code>，并在 Development 环境中允许它。在上述条件成立时，Development 可以允许访问；除非另有覆盖项，Production 会继承 Global 的 Deny。</p> <p>请确认已部署的执行器支持这些合并规则。常规受管执行器的覆盖情况仍需验证。</p> <p>协调器限制：云端运行时不支持受管 HTTP/SOCKS 监听端口和非环回代理监听器；socket 规则是否受支持取决于执行路径。下表中的本地执行设置不是受支持的 Orchestrator 配置。</p> <p><a id=\"other-network-runtime-limits\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_28\">其他网络运行时限制</h4> <p>下方所有字段均位于 <code>requirements.toml</code> 的 <code>experimental_network</code> 下。箭头表示同一策略中的 Global 值，再到环境值。这些本地执行案例并不能证明相同设置也受 Orchestrator 支持。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>设置及尝试的覆盖方式</th> <th>运行时行为</th> <th>管理员操作</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>domains：Deny → Allow</td> <td>在受支持的路径中，环境 Allow 会替换同一 Global 域名键。其他匹配的 Deny 规则仍然生效。</td> <td>在已部署的执行器上分别测试应允许和应阻止的请求。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>managed_allowed_domains_only</code>：true → false</td> <td>不要仅根据域名键的组合推断独占行为。若最终 <code>enabled = true</code> 且 <code>managed_allowed_domains_only = true</code>，普通命令必须有最终生效的 Allow 条目。</td> <td>检查最终生效的设置和继承的 Allow 条目。只有 Deny 的策略不会自动放行互联网其他部分。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>allow_local_binding</code>：false → true</td> <td>在受支持的 Codex Cloud 代理路径中，显式的 false 可能阻止访问上游代理。受支持的更高优先级 Cloud 覆盖项可以更改该值。</td> <td>检查最终值的来源以及执行器是否支持。不要把 Cloud 默认值套用到 Local。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>代理启用、独占模式、上游代理行为和 Unix socket 规则都可能因执行器而异。不要把域名键的组合规则泛化到这些设置。请核实最终生效值并测试已部署的运行时。有关 Codex Cloud 本地/私有网络连接，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">配置网络访问要求</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"verify-behavior-before-rollout\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_29\">推出前验证行为</h4> <p>请测试工作流实际使用的目标地址、本地监听器、代理行为和 socket 路径。在每个受影响的环境中，分别测试一项应当成功的操作和一项应当被阻止的操作。还要确认已部署的运行时版本支持你使用的每一项设置。</p> <p><a id=\"control-desktop-app-network-destinations\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_30\">控制桌面应用的网络目标</h3> <p>在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，于 Global 范围配置这些桌面专用要求。<strong>Network use controls</strong> 界面管理的是命令联网，属于另一项独立策略。</p> <p>使用 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中的 <code>[application.network]</code> 限制桌面应用的网络目标。设为 <code>enabled = true</code> 后，外部请求必须使用 HTTPS 或 WSS，并精确匹配 <code>[application.network.domains]</code> 中值为 <code>\"allow\"</code> 的域名。子域名不会被自动允许。域名映射为空时，不允许访问任何外部目标。有关受支持的键，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <p>此策略与命令网络和浏览器来源规则彼此独立。它不会对原生模块或派生进程施加目标地址限制，也不会管控 Work Cloud 执行。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-plugins-and-managed-hooks\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"hooks\">配置插件与受管 Hooks</h2> <p>对于以下任务，请在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，或使用受支持的本地配置来源。市场默认值、强制实施的插件限制和受管 Hooks 用途各不相同；请选择与所需策略相符的任务。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-plugin-marketplaces-and-defaults\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_31\">配置插件市场与默认值</h3> <p>使用 <strong>Agent security &gt; Edit as TOML</strong>，在 Global 范围的 <code>config.toml</code> 文档中定义本地或 Git 插件市场以及插件默认值。也可以通过系统级 <code>config.toml</code> 分发这些设置。它们提供的是市场和插件的初始配置，而非强制策略。若要限制成员可以使用哪些来源，请配置<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-plugin-marketplace-sources\">市场来源要求</a>。</p> <p>配置项请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>；覆盖优先级请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">配置优先级</a>；项目级配置请参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">仓库插件设置</a>。<a href=\"#/article/a-ab115ea47731f92a\">工作区 GitHub 导入与同步</a>是另一项独立功能。</p> <p><a id=\"control-plugin-availability\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_32\">控制插件是否可用</h3> <p>若要强制执行插件功能设置，请在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> 配置 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code>；对于设备管理的客户端，也可以通过本地要求配置分发。<strong>Defaults &gt; Apps and tools &gt; Apps/connectors</strong> 用于设置应用工具的默认值，并不是强制实施的 <code>features.plugins</code> 开关。有关工作区插件权限，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">Apps and connectors</a>。</p> <p>若要在受支持的本地客户端中关闭插件，请在 <code>features.plugins</code> 中将 <code>false</code> 设为 <code>requirements.toml</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">features.plugins = false\n</code></pre> <p>即使用户使用 API 密钥登录 Codex，此设置仍然适用。支持的配置请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>features.plugins</code> 参考</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"restrict-plugin-marketplace-sources\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_33\">限制插件市场来源</h3> <p>使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，在 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中配置市场来源要求，也可以通过本地要求配置分发。这些规则会限制用户可以添加和使用哪些本地或 Git 来源。它们与<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-plugin-marketplaces-and-defaults\">市场默认值</a>相互独立。</p> <p>若要限制插件市场来源，请设置 <code>restrict_to_allowed_sources = true</code>，并定义一条或多条来源规则：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[marketplaces]\nrestrict_to_allowed_sources = true\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.company_plugins]\nsource = \"git\"\nurl = \"https://github.com/example/company-plugins.git\"\nref = \"main\"\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.internal_git]\nsource = \"host_pattern\"\nhost_pattern = '^git\\.example\\.com$'\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.local_plugins]\nsource = \"local\"\npath = \"/opt/company/codex-plugins\"\n</code></pre> <p>Git 规则会匹配规范化后的仓库 URL；如果设置了 <code>ref</code>，也必须精确匹配。主机模式是与小写 Git 主机名匹配的正则表达式；如需完整匹配主机名，请使用 <code>^</code> 和 <code>$</code>。本地规则必须使用绝对且规范化的路径。完整结构和合并行为请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> 参考</a>。</p> <p>这些要求会拒绝不匹配来源规则的市场添加、插件安装和已配置 Git 市场刷新操作；运行时也会过滤已配置的市场及其中的插件。</p> <p>OpenAI 策划的 Git 插件市场（包括 API 密钥目录）也必须匹配来源允许列表。若要允许这些市场，请加入下面这个不限制 <code>ref</code> 的 Git 来源：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[marketplaces.allowed_sources.openai_curated]\nsource = \"git\"\nurl = \"https://github.com/openai/plugins.git\"\n</code></pre> <p>若要排除这些策划目录，请省略该来源，并确保没有更宽泛的主机规则允许它。捆绑插件和从远程安装的工作区插件不受这项 Git 来源策略管理。</p> <p>这些来源限制只适用于支持插件市场操作的本地客户端：桌面应用中的 ChatGPT 与 Codex，以及 Codex CLI。它们不会控制网页版或移动版 ChatGPT 中的插件使用，也不会将插件添加到 IDE 扩展中。</p> <p><a id=\"enforce-managed-hooks-from-requirements\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"requirements-hooks\">从 requirements 强制执行受管 Hooks</h3> <p>受管 Hooks 会在受支持的任务或工具事件发生时运行管理员定义的操作。在 Agent security 中使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，于 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中配置它们；设置控件没有专用的 Hooks 编辑器。下方介绍的执行路径决定了哪些类型的 Hook 可以运行。</p> <p>启用受管策略和远程 Hooks 后，带本地访问功能的 Work Cloud 与 dots 会在云端协调器上使用管理员管理的远程 MCP Hooks。请在 Global <code>requirements.toml</code> 中配置 <code>mcp_tool</code> 处理程序。不带本地访问功能的 Work Cloud 和个人账户不使用这些企业级 Hooks。即使工具在本地执行，云端协调也不支持命令/Shell、提示词和智能体处理程序；也不支持来自本地配置、插件或本地目录的 Hooks、限定环境的 Hooks，以及 <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP Hooks。若协调和执行都在本地进行，仅限本地的 Work 和 Codex 线程仍可使用现有受支持的 Hooks。管理员仍可在这些工作流对应的 Agent Security 中配置受支持的受管 Hooks。</p> <p>依赖这些 Hooks 之前，请测试回调连接、所需事件和故障行为。受支持的明确拒绝可以阻止操作；但 <code>PreToolUse</code> 回调发生错误、超时或返回格式错误的响应时，Hook 可能失败而不会阻止工具运行。MCP Hooks 不会提供完整的 Compliance API 审计轨迹。下方的脚本和目录示例仅适用于 Codex 工作流。</p> <p>管理员也可以直接在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中定义受管生命周期 Hooks。Hook 配置本身放在 <code>[hooks]</code> 中；<code>managed_dir</code> 应指向 MDM 或终端管理工具安装相关脚本的目录。</p> <p>若要确保即使用户在本地关闭了 Hooks，受管 Hooks 仍会运行，请在 <code>[features].hooks = true</code> 旁边固定 <code>[hooks]</code>。如果要跳过用户、项目、会话和插件来源的 Hooks，同时仍允许受管 Hooks，请设置 <code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code>。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_managed_hooks_only = true\n\n[features]\nhooks = true\n\n[hooks]\nmanaged_dir = \"/enterprise/hooks\"\nwindows_managed_dir = 'C:\\enterprise\\hooks'\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\nmatcher = \"^Bash$\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\ntype = \"command\"\ncommand = \"python3 /enterprise/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"\ncommand_windows = 'py -3 C:\\enterprise\\hooks\\pre_tool_use_policy.py'\ntimeout = 30\nstatusMessage = \"Checking managed Bash command\"\n</code></pre> <p>注意：</p> <ul> <li>本地运行环境会强制执行 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中的 Hook 配置，但不会分发 <code>managed_dir</code> 中的脚本。</li> <li>请通过 MDM 或设备管理方案分发这些脚本。</li> <li>受管 Hook 命令应引用受管目录下的绝对脚本路径。</li> <li><code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code> 会跳过用户、项目、会话和插件来源的 Hooks，但仍会加载 <code>requirements.toml</code> 及其他受管配置层中的 Hooks。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"understand-policy-scope-and-precedence\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_34\">了解策略适用范围与优先级</h2> <p><a id=\"agent-security-and-work-sync\"></a></p> <p>本节介绍如何检查策略的适用位置、要求如何组合、客户端如何接收云端管理的设置，以及怎样验证最终结果。</p> <p>对于启用了本地访问的 Work 和 dots，在启用受管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略会管控共享云端协调器；适用的本地执行要求则管控已连接的计算机。Work 云端容器保留现有 Work Cloud 策略；受支持的设备控制项仍适用于本地执行。Codex 保持现有配置行为。受管配置不会授予工作区席位或功能访问权限；有关这些控制项，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">角色与工作区权限</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"agent-security-and-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"agent-security-work-cloud\">Agent Security 与 Work Cloud 本地计算机访问</h3> <p>在管理控制台中使用 Agent Security 管理策略与配置。它取代了 Policies &amp; Configuration。Agent Security 可在管理控制台中使用，与是否启用 Local computer access with Work Cloud 无关。现有策略、分配关系和排序均会保留；请在 Agent Security 中检查现有策略。Local computer access with Work Cloud 是一项单独选择加入的功能。迁移说明请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a>。</p> <p>将策略自动化迁移到 <strong>Agent Security</strong> 之前：</p> <ol> <li>识别受影响的集成。盘点会更新策略的 Terraform 配置和脚本，并记录它们管理哪些策略。</li> <li>使用策略 API 管理 Global 设置。若要管理 Local 或 Codex Cloud 设置，请使用 Agent Security 界面。迁移后，现有 Global API 工作流仍可使用。请测试脚本和 Terraform 集成，并确认策略分配关系及排序没有改变。</li> <li>完成必要的更改并测试自动化。确认预期更新会应用到正确的 Agent Security 策略，并且相应控制确实得到强制执行。</li> </ol> <p>迁移策略不会为 Work 或 dots 授予本地计算机访问权限。建议在推出前检查或创建策略，但启用访问权限的确认流程并不要求先创建策略。启用 <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> 之前，请检查迁移后的基线；如果你的策略目前只通过 MDM 下发，请先创建云端基线。如果任一云端策略启用了 <code>enforce_residency</code>，Work 和 dots 的 <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> 都会被禁用。这项保护措施不会配置工作区数据驻留，也不会单独禁用 Work Cloud 或 dots。</p> <p>环境覆盖项用于在同一策略内为不同环境设置不同的受支持选项。同一策略中，优先顺序从高到低为：按操作系统区分的环境覆盖项 → 适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖项 → Global。跨策略时，高优先级策略仍优先，即使低优先级策略更具体也一样。部分强制要求有字段专属的合并规则。请确认工作区实际提供哪些覆盖项编辑器和字段。</p> <p>启用受管策略和远程 Hooks 后，带本地访问功能的 Work Cloud 与 dots 会在云端协调器上使用管理员管理的远程 MCP Hooks。请在 Global <code>requirements.toml</code> 中配置 <code>mcp_tool</code> 处理程序。不带本地访问功能的 Work Cloud 和个人账户不使用这些企业级 Hooks。即使工具在本地执行，云端协调也不支持命令/Shell、提示词和智能体处理程序；也不支持来自本地配置、插件或本地目录的 Hooks、限定环境的 Hooks，以及 <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP Hooks。若协调和执行都在本地进行，仅限本地的 Work 和 Codex 线程仍可使用现有受支持的 Hooks。管理员仍可在这些工作流对应的 Agent Security 中配置受支持的受管 Hooks。依赖这些 Hooks 之前，请测试回调连接、所需事件和故障行为。受支持的明确拒绝可以阻止操作；但 <code>PreToolUse</code> 回调发生错误、超时或返回格式错误的响应时，Hook 可能失败而不会阻止工具运行。MCP Hooks 不会提供完整的 Compliance API 审计轨迹。有关配置字段，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"locations-and-precedence\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_35\">文件位置与优先级</h3> <p>在本地执行时，强制要求按以下顺序从低到高应用。Work Cloud 本地计算机访问中的本地步骤也遵循此顺序：</p> <ol> <li>系统级 <code>requirements.toml</code>（Unix 系统，包括 Linux 和 macOS：<code>/etc/codex/requirements.toml</code>；Windows：<code>%ProgramData%\\OpenAI\\Codex\\requirements.toml</code>）。</li> <li>通过云端配置包下发的 Agent Security 要求。</li> <li>本地客户端重新解释为强制要求的旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 字段。</li> <li>通过 <code>com.openai.codex:requirements_toml_base64</code> 下发的 macOS 受管偏好设置（MDM）。</li> </ol> <p>高优先级层会覆盖低优先级层中的普通标量值和列表值。表格按键合并；但规则、Hooks 和文件系统限制等要求有各自专门的组合方式。请以最新的<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> 参考</a>为准，不要假设所有字段都会以相同方式合并。</p> <p>为保持向后兼容，受支持的本地客户端会把旧版 <code>approval_policy</code>、<code>approvals_reviewer</code> 和 <code>sandbox_mode</code> 字段重新解释为强制要求。转换时可能会加入兼容性选项；如需明确指定允许列表，请使用 <code>requirements.toml</code>。</p> <p><a id=\"precedence-for-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"work-cloud\">Work Cloud 本地计算机访问的策略优先级</h3> <p>对于启用了本地访问的 Work 和 dots，请区分 Global 协调器策略与执行策略。适用的本地要求管控已连接的计算机。Work 云端容器和 dots 云端计算机会使用各自的执行配置与强制要求，而不是其他执行器所用的受管环境配置包。</p> <p>在本地执行时，MDM 和旧版受管设备要求的优先级高于 Agent Security；设备的系统级 requirements 文件优先级低于 Agent Security。同一策略中，按操作系统区分的环境覆盖项优先于适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖项，后者又优先于 Global。跨策略时，策略优先级高于具体程度。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>控制项</th> <th>不使用同步的本地 Work 与本地 Codex</th> <th>Work Cloud 本地计算机访问</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>企业要求</td> <td>遵循上文的本地要求顺序。</td> <td>应用于本地执行器。Work 云端容器保留现有 Work Cloud 策略。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>设备执行限制</td> <td>由受支持的本地控制项强制执行。</td> <td>遵循上文的本地要求顺序，包括 MDM 和旧版要求优先于 Agent Security。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>环境覆盖项</td> <td>使用相应产品的配置模型。</td> <td>同一策略中依次采用按操作系统区分的覆盖项、适用于所有操作系统的覆盖项、Global。更低优先级策略不能凭借更具体而胜出。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>强制要求用于设置限制，配置默认值用于提供初始设置。对于启用了本地访问的 Work 和 dots，启用受管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略会通过共享云端协调器生效。适用的本地 <code>requirements.toml</code> 要求管控已连接计算机上的执行。Work 云端容器和 dots 云端计算机会使用各自的执行配置与强制要求，而不是其他执行器所用的受管环境配置包。本地执行限制不会自动应用到这些云端计算机。请检查云端功能权限，并分别测试本地和云端执行。</p> <p>包括审批和网页搜索在内的协调器控制项应放在 Global 中。有相应界面时，使用专门的 Allowed approval policies 和 Allowed web search modes 控制项；其他受支持字段则使用 TOML。字段清单和执行范围请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"cloud-managed-requirements\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_36\">云端受管要求</h3> <p>用户使用受支持的方案登录 ChatGPT 后，受支持的本地客户端可以接收与其工作区关联、由管理员强制执行的要求。这是一种下发兼容 <code>requirements.toml</code> 策略的渠道，不会授予工作区访问权限，也不会取代工作区 RBAC。身份验证要求必须<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">在本地管理</a>。</p> <p>使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-a-policy-in-agent-security\">Agent security 控件</a>配置常见要求。若要只允许按需审批，并仅使用只读或工作区可写沙盒，请在 <strong>Policy guardrails</strong> 下使用 <strong>Approval policies</strong> 和 <strong>Sandbox modes</strong>。若还要在受支持的 Shell 入口点执行前提示，请使用 <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>，将 <code>[rules]</code> 表添加到 Global 范围的 <code>requirements.toml</code>。</p> <p>完整的 TOML 等效配置如下：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ any_of = [\"bash\", \"sh\", \"zsh\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require explicit approval for shell entry points\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>请确认每个受管客户端版本都支持你选择的键。将策略分配给目标用户或群组后，核实策略已在其受支持的客户端中生效。当前配置格式请参阅配置参考；当前的分配行为请参阅管理界面。</p> <p>服务会选择适用于已登录身份的企业受管要求层。本地客户端再将这些要求与<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">文件位置与优先级</a>中介绍的其他要求来源一起评估。请使用当前管理界面创建并分配工作区策略。不要依赖复制来的组匹配算法；这项行为由管理服务负责，可能会独立于本地要求格式而变化。</p> <p>受支持的键和示例请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=example-requirementstoml\">requirements.toml 示例</a>以及<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">配置参考中的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 一节</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"how-local-clients-apply-cloud-managed-requirements\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_37\">本地客户端如何应用云端受管要求</h4> <p>用户在受支持的方案下登录 ChatGPT 并启动受支持的本地客户端时，客户端会先检查是否有与该身份匹配且仍有效的缓存。如果没有可用的有效缓存，客户端会重试获取适用的配置包；成功后会写入带签名的缓存条目。如果请求失败或超时，并且没有有效缓存，云端配置包加载就会报错，而不会在缺少云端受管要求的情况下悄悄启动。</p> <p>解析缓存后，客户端会将云端要求与上文介绍的其他要求层组合。后台刷新可以更新缓存，供下次启动使用；它不会替换当前进程已经加载的要求。</p> <p>缓存解析完成后，客户端会将云端要求与上文所述的其他要求层组合起来。后台刷新会更新缓存的配置包，使后续读取配置时可以使用新内容。但这不保证正在运行的任务会立即采用已变更的策略。部分桌面设置需要重启应用；请遵循重启提示，并在依赖这些变更之前确认实际生效的设置。</p> <p><a id=\"confirm-the-admin-and-employee-experience\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_38\">核对管理员和员工的实际体验</h3> <p>为每项受管策略指定负责人，记录应接收该策略的用户或群组，并说明施加文件系统、网络、审批或权限配置方案限制的业务原因。</p> <p>请使用拥有预期权限的用户，测试一项允许的工作流和一项应被阻止的工作流。检查受支持客户端中的最终生效设置。仅凭工作区角色或群组并不能强制执行本地运行时限制。</p> <p>使用 Agent security 中的 <strong>Test policies</strong>，检查哪些策略会匹配某位成员、某个群组或某项角色选择。在可编辑会话中，该功能检查的是当前草稿，不会保存草稿。结果展示的是策略选择情况，并非最终合并后的配置，也不代表客户端一定会强制执行某项限制。保存后，请在每种相关客户端和执行路径上测试允许与阻止的工作流。</p> <p><a id=\"deploy-device-managed-configuration\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_39\">部署设备管理配置</h2> <p>身份验证限制和设备管理策略应使用本地系统要求或 MDM。旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 机制的启动优先级与 Agent Security 中的 <strong>Defaults</strong> 不同。MDM 工作流涵盖要求配置和旧版默认值。</p> <p><a id=\"manage-authentication-locally\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_40\">在本地管理身份验证</h3> <p>在系统级 <code>requirements.toml</code> 文件或 macOS MDM 要求中设置 <code>allowed_login_methods</code>、<code>allowed_chatgpt_workspaces</code>、<code>cli_auth_credentials_store</code> 和 <code>chatgpt_base_url</code>。Codex 会忽略云端受管要求中的这四个字段。本地身份验证要求会在加载凭据以及 Codex 获取云端策略之前应用。</p> <p>若要要求用户登录获批的 ChatGPT 工作区，并将凭据保存在操作系统的凭据存储区，请使用：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]\nallowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"]\ncli_auth_credentials_store = \"keyring\"\n</code></pre> <p><code>allowed_login_methods</code> 可设为 <code>chatgpt</code>、<code>api</code> 或两者。如果省略此项，就不会限制登录方式；如果设置了此项，列表至少必须包含一种方式。<code>api</code> 允许使用 API 身份验证，包括 Amazon Bedrock。工作区限制也适用于 <a href=\"#/article/a-f5bc151215cdfb88\">Codex 访问令牌</a>。</p> <p>用户配置的 <code>forced_login_method</code> 和 <code>forced_chatgpt_workspace_id</code> 必须符合这些要求。用户选择的工作区也必须出现在受管工作区允许列表中。如果没有任何工作区匹配，就无法使用 ChatGPT 登录；在策略允许时，仍可使用 API 身份验证。如果没有可用的登录方式，Codex 将拒绝启动。</p> <p>凭据存储模式和服务 URL 配置请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">要求配置参考</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"managed-defaults-managed_configtoml\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"managed_configtoml\">受管默认值（<code>managed_config.toml</code>）</h3> <p>对于受支持的云端初始值，请使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">Agent security Defaults</a>。本节介绍旧版设备管理机制 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 及其不同的启动优先级。在 Agent security 中编辑 <strong>Defaults</strong>，不会向成员设备部署 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 文件。</p> <p>受管默认值用于设置受支持的本地客户端启动时采用的配置。启动时，它们会覆盖用户本地 <code>config.toml</code> 和 CLI <code>--config</code> 覆盖项。用户仍可在本次运行中更改设置；客户端下次启动时会再次应用默认值。</p> <p>GPT-5.5 将于 2026 年 10 月 14 日太平洋夏令时间中午（UTC 19:00）起，不再支持通过 ChatGPT 登录的 Codex。如果这些用户的受管默认值、macOS MDM 配置描述文件或已保存配置将模型固定为 <code>gpt-5.5</code>，请替换为可用模型。管理员为相关用户启用 <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> 后，可选用该模型。通过 OpenAI API 或使用 API 密钥进行身份验证的 Codex 会话不受影响。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb?anchor=prepare-for-the-gpt-55-retirement\">工作区模型可用性</a>。</p> <p>如果配置仍固定使用 <code>gpt-5.4</code> 或 <code>gpt-5.4-mini</code>，请遵循 <a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb?anchor=prepare-for-the-gpt-54-retirement\">GPT-5.4 迁移指南</a>。</p> <p>请确认受管默认值符合你的要求；本地运行环境会拒绝不允许的值。</p> <p><a id=\"precedence-and-layering\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_41\">优先级与配置层</h4> <p>本地运行环境按以下顺序组合最终生效的配置（上方覆盖下方）：</p> <ul> <li>受管偏好设置（macOS MDM；最高优先级）</li> <li><code>managed_config.toml</code>（系统或受管文件）</li> <li><code>config.toml</code>（用户的基础配置）</li> </ul> <p>CLI 的 <code>--config key=value</code> 覆盖项会作用于基础配置，但受管层会覆盖它们。因此，即使你提供了本地命令行标志，每次运行仍会从受管默认值开始。</p> <p>云端 <code>config.toml</code> 使用<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">常规配置优先级</a>，而不是上文所述的旧版顺序。云端 <code>requirements.toml</code> 使用<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">强制要求优先级</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"locations\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_42\">文件位置</h4> <ul> <li>Linux/macOS（Unix）：<code>/etc/codex/managed_config.toml</code></li> <li>Windows/非 Unix：<code>~/.codex/managed_config.toml</code></li> </ul> <p>如果文件不存在，本地运行环境就会跳过受管配置层。</p> <p><a id=\"example-managed_configtoml\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"managed_configtoml_1\">managed_config.toml 示例</h4> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Set conservative defaults\napproval_policy = \"on-request\"\nsandbox_mode    = \"workspace-write\"\n\n[sandbox_workspace_write]\nnetwork_access = false             # keep network disabled unless explicitly allowed\n\n[otel]\nenvironment = \"prod\"\nexporter = \"otlp-http\"            # point at your collector\nlog_user_prompt = false            # keep prompts redacted\n# exporter details live under exporter tables; see Monitoring and telemetry above\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"recommended-guardrails\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_43\">建议的安全护栏</h4> <ul> <li>对大多数用户，优先使用带审批的 <code>workspace-write</code>；完全访问权限只用于受控容器。</li> <li>除非安全审查允许访问遥测收集器或工作流所需域名，否则保持 <code>network_access = false</code>。</li> <li>使用受管配置固定 OTel 设置（导出器、环境）；除非策略明确允许保存提示词内容，否则保持 <code>log_user_prompt = false</code>。</li> <li>定期审查本地 <code>config.toml</code> 与受管策略之间的差异，及时发现配置漂移；受管层应优先于本地标志和文件。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"macos-managed-preferences-mdm\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"macos-mdm\">macOS 受管偏好设置（MDM）</h3> <p>在 macOS 上，管理员可以推送设备配置描述文件，并在以下位置提供经过 Base64 编码的 TOML 内容：</p> <ul> <li>偏好设置域：<code>com.openai.codex</code></li> <li>键：</li> <li><code>config_toml_base64</code>（受管默认值）</li> <li><code>requirements_toml_base64</code>（强制要求）</li> </ul> <p>本地运行时会将这些“受管偏好设置”载荷解析为 TOML。对于受管默认值（<code>config_toml_base64</code>），受管偏好设置具有最高优先级；对于要求配置（<code>requirements_toml_base64</code>），优先级遵循<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">要求配置顺序</a>。同一张要求配置侧的 <code>[features]</code> 表也适用于 <code>requirements_toml_base64</code>；在那里同样要使用规范的功能键。</p> <p><a id=\"mdm-setup-workflow\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"mdm\">MDM 设置流程</h4> <p>本地运行环境遵循标准 macOS MDM 内容，因此你可以使用 <code>Jamf Pro</code>、<code>Fleet</code> 或 <code>Kandji</code> 等工具分发设置。一个简化的部署流程如下：</p> <ol> <li>编写受管负载 TOML，并使用 <code>base64</code> 编码（不要换行）。</li> <li>将编码后的字符串放入 MDM 配置描述文件的 <code>com.openai.codex</code> 域下：受管默认值使用 <code>config_toml_base64</code>，强制要求使用 <code>requirements_toml_base64</code>。</li> <li>推送配置描述文件，然后让用户重启受支持的本地客户端，并确认启动时的配置摘要显示了受管值。</li> <li>撤销或更改策略时，更新受管负载；客户端下次启动时会读取更新后的偏好设置。</li> </ol> <p>不要在负载中嵌入机密或频繁变化的动态值。应像管理其他 MDM 设置一样，在变更控制流程下管理受管 TOML。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"managed-configuration\">Managed configuration</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Managed configuration lets enterprise admins set supported requirements and defaults for ChatGPT Work and Codex. Requirements constrain what users and tasks can do. Defaults provide starting values. Support depends on the product, client version, and execution environment.</p> <p>Start in <strong>Agent security</strong> in the Admin Console for supported cloud-managed settings. Use the settings controls for common tasks, or select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to inspect, copy, or edit the configuration text. Both views edit the same policy; TOML also exposes supported fields without a dedicated control. You don't need to distribute a local file to use cloud-managed settings. Authentication restrictions and device-managed deployments have separate <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">local setup</a>.</p> <p>Enterprise admins can control supported local client behavior with:</p> <ul> <li> <p><strong>Requirements</strong>: admin-enforced constraints that users can't override.</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>Configuration defaults</strong>: system or cloud-managed <code>config.toml</code> settings that users can override.</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>Legacy managed defaults</strong>: <code>managed_config.toml</code> starting values applied when a supported client launches. Users can still change settings during a run. The client reapplies these defaults the next time it starts.</p> </li> </ul> <p>See <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a> for the Global baseline, environment overrides, and orchestrator and executor field lists.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-a-policy-in-agent-security\">Configure a policy in Agent security</h2> <p>Where Agent security is enabled, workspace owners, workspace admins, and members with Codex admin permissions can manage policies. Access to the policy editor doesn't grant members access to the features that a policy controls.</p> <p>Use <strong>Workspace default</strong> for the workspace baseline. Use <strong>Overrides</strong> when specific users, groups, or roles need different settings. An audience override selects who receives a policy; an environment setting selects where supported execution controls apply.</p> <ol> <li>Open <strong>Admin Console</strong> &gt; <strong>Agent security</strong> and select <strong>Workspace default</strong> or the policy you want to edit under <strong>Overrides</strong>.</li> <li>Open <strong>Requirements</strong> to set enforced limits, or <strong>Defaults</strong> to set starting values that members can change within those limits.</li> <li>Keep approval, web search, and other agent-wide settings in <strong>Global</strong>. Where available, use <strong>Add environment settings</strong> in the relevant card to add <strong>Local Environments</strong> or <strong>Codex Cloud Environments</strong> for supported execution settings. An empty environment document inherits settings from Global; it doesn't copy or pin the current baseline.</li> <li>Use the controls described in this guide. For advanced fields, select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> and edit the appropriate scope's <code>requirements.toml</code> or <code>config.toml</code>. Select <strong>Use settings controls</strong> to return to the visual editor.</li> <li>Review validation messages, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>. Applying a rule in a rule editor updates the draft; it doesn't save the policy by itself.</li> <li>Check policy selection and the supported client behavior as described in <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=confirm-the-admin-and-employee-experience\">Confirm the admin and employee experience</a>.</li> </ol> <p>Environment editors and supported fields depend on the workspace and policy. See <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=where-settings-apply\">Where settings apply</a> before moving a Global setting into an environment. A setting in Global doesn't necessarily apply to every product or execution path.</p> <h3 id=\"copy-or-reuse-a-configuration\">Copy or reuse a configuration</h3> <p>The visual editor doesn't replace TOML. Select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to copy a configuration, keep a reference copy, or reuse settings in another policy. Select <strong>Use settings controls</strong> to switch back to the visual editor.</p> <ol> <li>Open the source policy and select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>.</li> <li>Choose the scope you want to copy: <strong>Global</strong> or an available environment tab. Copy the text from <strong>Requirements</strong> (<code>requirements.toml</code>), <strong>Defaults</strong> (<code>config.toml</code>), or both, depending on what you want to reuse.</li> <li>Open the destination policy, select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>, and choose the matching scope. Paste requirements into <code>requirements.toml</code> and defaults into <code>config.toml</code>. Review existing destination settings before replacing text; keep unrelated settings that should still apply.</li> <li>Review validation messages and the destination policy's targets and priority, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>. Confirm the resulting client behavior before relying on the copied policy.</li> </ol> <p>The copied text contains the authored settings for that policy and scope. It doesn't include assignments, policy order, other environment documents, or the effective configuration combined from other policies and device settings. Copy environment settings separately when needed. Switching views or copying text doesn't save a policy change.</p> <p>Try the example below: change a setting, then select <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to see its configuration. Switch back with <strong>Use settings controls</strong> to keep experimenting.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Interactive example of Agent security settings and their TOML representation. The initial requirements allow on-request approvals, read-only sandboxing, and disabled web search. Change a sample setting and select Edit as TOML to see the corresponding field; Use settings controls returns to the same sample choices. This example does not change workspace policies.</p> </blockquote> <h3 id=\"choose-the-right-control\">Choose the right control</h3> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Task</th> <th>Where to configure it</th> <th>What it changes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">Restrict browser and native-app access</a></td> <td><strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Desktop feature availability</strong>, <strong>Site rules</strong>, and <strong>App rules</strong></td> <td>Controls which supported desktop capabilities are available and which sites or apps they can access.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\">Limit approval, sandbox, or web search choices</a></td> <td><strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Policy guardrails</strong></td> <td>Restricts the choices available to supported clients.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">Set starting approval, permission, search, or tool behavior</a></td> <td><strong>Defaults</strong></td> <td>Sets values members can change within enforced requirements.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access\">Restrict command network destinations</a></td> <td><strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Network use controls</strong></td> <td>Applies managed networking rules to supported command traffic. It isn't a workspace-wide internet switch.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-plugins-and-managed-hooks\">Configure plugins and managed hooks</a></td> <td><strong>Edit as TOML</strong></td> <td>Lets you configure supported policy fields that have no dedicated visual control.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">Restrict login methods or workspaces before sign-in</a></td> <td>Local system requirements or macOS MDM</td> <td>Applies authentication restrictions before cloud policy is loaded.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"control-browser-and-computer-use\">Control browser and Computer Use</h2> <p>Use <strong>Agent security</strong> in the Admin Console to manage browser and native-app access for supported desktop clients. Start with the UI controls. The TOML examples below provide a configuration reference for file-based policy delivery and settings that need the TOML editor.</p> <p>Browser Use controls agent-driven activity in a browser. Computer Use controls agent-driven activity in native desktop apps. Settings for the built-in browser pane are separate from Browser Use. See <a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">browser sessions and existing sign-ins</a> for the differences between browser paths.</p> <h3 id=\"before-you-begin\">Before you begin</h3> <p>Use an account with permission to manage Agent Security policies. Confirm which people receive the policy and which client versions and operating systems you need to support. Review <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a> for policy assignments, Global settings, and supported environment overrides.</p> <p>Use <strong>Requirements</strong> for limits users can't override. <strong>Defaults</strong> set starting values within those limits. Allowing a site or app doesn't install a plugin, grant operating-system permissions, sign a user in, or approve an action that still requires review.</p> <p>Under <strong>Desktop feature availability</strong>, review <strong>Browser use</strong>, <strong>External browser use</strong>, and <strong>Computer use</strong>. <strong>Force off</strong> disables the corresponding feature; an allow rule for a site or app doesn't override it. Review <strong>Full CDP access</strong> separately; <strong>Block user opt-in</strong> prevents users from enabling it.</p> <p>These desktop controls don't configure the hosted Work Cloud browser. Manage <strong>Cloud browser use</strong> and <strong>Cloud network access</strong> separately under <strong>Admin Console &gt; Permissions &amp; roles &gt; Workspace capabilities &gt; Cloud computer capabilities</strong>. See <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\">how Agent Security applies to Work Cloud</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-browser-access\">Configure browser access</h3> <ol> <li>Open <strong>Admin Console &gt; Agent security</strong>. Use <strong>Workspace default</strong> for the workspace baseline, or open the relevant policy under <strong>Overrides</strong>. In <strong>Requirements</strong>, go to <strong>Desktop browser controls</strong>.</li> <li>In <strong>Site rules</strong>, open the <strong>Default</strong> row's actions and choose <strong>Edit Default</strong>. For access limited to approved sites, set <strong>Site access</strong> to <strong>Block</strong>, then choose <strong>Apply rule</strong>. The <strong>Default</strong> row supplies values that a matching site rule leaves at <strong>Use default rule</strong>.</li> <li>Choose <strong>Add site rule</strong>. In <strong>Site or pattern</strong>, enter an origin: a scheme, hostname, and optional port. For example, <code>https://example.com</code> is valid; <code>https://example.com/files</code> isn't. Don't include a path, query, or fragment. HTTP and HTTPS are different origins. Specify the port when it differs from the scheme's default.</li> <li>Set <strong>Site access</strong> and review <strong>Downloads</strong>, <strong>Uploads</strong>, and <strong>Debug/CDP</strong>. Choose <strong>Allow</strong> or <strong>Block</strong> as appropriate, or leave a setting at <strong>Use default rule</strong>. <strong>Debug/CDP</strong> controls full Chrome DevTools Protocol access for browser debugging. <strong>Allow</strong> lets normal policy and approval checks continue.</li> <li>Review <strong>Auto-review</strong>, <strong>Always allow</strong>, and <strong>Approval duration</strong> for the site. <strong>Approval duration</strong> offers <strong>This turn</strong> and <strong>This thread</strong>. Review <strong>Browser history</strong>, <strong>Save site approvals</strong>, and <strong>Auto-approve requests</strong> in <strong>Desktop browser controls</strong> as well.</li> <li>Choose <strong>Add rule</strong>, review validation messages, and select <strong>Save changes</strong>. Test an allowed site and a blocked site on a supported client that receives the policy. Test uploads, downloads, and other restricted actions separately.</li> </ol> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Agent Security site-rule editor with site access, transfers, debugging, and approval controls.</p> </blockquote> <p>Disabling automatic review for browser actions sends those actions to the user for approval instead. It doesn't disable model safety monitoring.</p> <p>When several origin patterns match, the most restrictive value wins for each setting. Local configuration can't relax a managed denial. Managed network denies and exclusive allowlists also apply to browser tools. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">browser policy fields</a> for origin patterns and approval behavior.</p> <p>Computer Use can also operate a browser as a native app. Browser Use site and capability rules don't apply to that path. Review native-app access separately; see the <a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">browser security boundaries</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-native-app-access\">Configure native-app access</h3> <ol> <li>In <strong>Requirements</strong>, go to <strong>Desktop app controls &gt; App rules</strong>.</li> <li>Use the <strong>Default</strong> row's actions to configure access for apps without a matching rule. To limit Computer Use to approved apps, block access by default and add allow rules for those apps.</li> <li>Choose <strong>Add app rule</strong>. In the <strong>Edit rule</strong> dialog, select <strong>macOS bundle ID</strong>, <strong>Windows AUMID</strong>, or <strong>Windows executable</strong> from <strong>App</strong>. Enter the exact <strong>App identifier</strong> for a bundle ID or Application User Model ID (AUMID). For <strong>Windows executable</strong>, enter <strong>Publisher name</strong> and <strong>Product name</strong>; <strong>Binary name</strong> is optional. Use verified app identity information, not a display name or file path.</li> <li>Set <strong>App access</strong> for the rule, then choose <strong>Add rule</strong>. Review <strong>Save app approvals</strong> under <strong>Desktop app controls</strong>, review validation messages, and select <strong>Save changes</strong>. Disabling saved approvals still permits approvals for the current session; allowing saved approvals doesn't approve an app.</li> <li>Test an allowed app and a blocked app on each supported operating system in your deployment. Confirm that operating-system permissions still apply and that users must still provide required approvals.</li> </ol> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Agent Security native-app rule editor showing macOS bundle ID, app identifier, and app access.</p> </blockquote> <p>See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">Computer Use policy fields</a> for Windows identity requirements and <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-locked-computer-use\">locked-use restrictions</a> for managed Macs.</p> <h3 id=\"check-the-effective-policy\">Check the effective policy</h3> <p>If an allowed site or app is still blocked, check other matching rules, policy assignments and priority, device requirements, and user configuration. An allow rule in one source doesn't bypass another applicable denial. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">requirements locations and precedence</a>.</p> <p>If a setting has no effect, verify that the client, operating system, and policy delivery path support it. Pay particular attention to Work Cloud with local computer access: some fields have partial support or require validation. See <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Work compatibility in the Configuration Reference</a>.</p> <p>Check <a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">workspace permissions</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">plugin controls</a> separately when a user can't access the feature or required plugin.</p> <h3 id=\"toml-configuration-reference\">TOML configuration reference</h3> <p>The following examples describe the same policy intent for <code>requirements.toml</code>. Use them when managing policy through files or the TOML editor. You don't need to edit a local file to use the controls available in Agent Security.</p> <p>This browser example denies access by default, allows <code>https://example.com</code>, blocks uploads and full CDP access there, and limits site-access approval to the current turn:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[browser_use]\nallow_history_access = false\nallow_global_persistent_approval = false\n\n[browser_use.default_origin_policy]\naccess = \"deny\"\n\n[browser_use.origins.\"https://example.com\"]\naccess = \"allow\"\nuploads = \"deny\"\ndownloads = \"allow\"\nfull_cdp_access = \"deny\"\npersistent_approval = false\naccess_approval_lifetime = \"turn\"\n</code></pre> <p>To disable automatic review for browser actions, set <code>browser_use.disable_auto_review = true</code>, or set <code>auto_review = \"deny\"</code> on an origin policy to restrict it for that origin.</p> <p>To enable <a href=\"#/article/a-d8e7ab2e8f18b498\">Site tools</a> for an eligible Enterprise workspace, set <code>browser_use.allow_webmcp = true</code>. This managed opt-in applies to the desktop app's built-in browser. Setting it to <code>false</code> or omitting it leaves normal feature availability checks in place; <code>false</code> does not enforce a block. User preferences and site permissions still apply.</p> <p>This native-app example allows Calculator on macOS, denies other apps by default, and prevents app approvals from persisting across sessions:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[computer_use]\ndefault_app_access = \"deny\"\nallow_persistent_approval = false\n\n[computer_use.macos.bundle_ids]\n\"com.apple.calculator\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>Windows policies can identify packaged apps with <code>computer_use.windows.aumids</code> or executables with <code>computer_use.windows.exes</code>. Executable rules require <code>publisher_name</code>, <code>product_name</code>, and <code>access</code>; <code>binary_name</code> is optional.</p> <p>For all supported fields and values, use the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> reference</a>. To disable a capability rather than restrict individual sites or apps, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=pin-feature-flags\">Pin feature flags</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"other-desktop-controls\">Other desktop controls</h3> <p>The following desktop controls cover feature availability, Locked Use, Appshots, and device remote control. Use dedicated settings for feature availability and Locked Use; configure Appshots and device remote control with <strong>Edit as TOML</strong>. For destination restrictions on desktop-app traffic, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-desktop-app-network-destinations\">Control desktop app network destinations</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"pin-feature-flags\">Pin feature flags</h4> <p>For desktop features exposed in Agent security, open <strong>Global</strong> &gt; <strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Desktop feature availability</strong>. Set <strong>In-app browser</strong>, <strong>Browser use</strong>, <strong>External browser use</strong>, or <strong>Computer use</strong> to <strong>Force off</strong> to disable that capability, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>. For <strong>Full CDP access</strong>, choose <strong>Block user opt-in</strong> to prevent users from enabling it. Allowing a feature doesn't override its product, platform, or rollout availability.</p> <p>For other supported <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=feature-flags\">feature flags</a>, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> and the Global <code>requirements.toml</code> document, or a supported local requirements source. The following examples include both runtime and desktop-specific feature pins:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[features]\nhooks = true\nunified_exec = false\n\n# Disable surface-specific features when needed.\nbrowser_use = false\nbrowser_use_full_cdp_access = false\nbrowser_use_external = false\nin_app_browser = false\nin_app_updates = false\ncomputer_use = false\n</code></pre> <p>Use the canonical feature keys from <code>config.toml</code>'s <code>[features]</code> table for runtime features. The local runtime normalizes recognized features to meet these pins and rejects conflicting writes to <code>config.toml</code> or profile file feature settings.</p> <p>A managed <code>unified_exec = false</code> keeps command execution available, but disables resumable processes and the <code>write_stdin</code> tool. User configuration can't disable this feature.</p> <p><a id=\"disable-codex-feature-surfaces\"></a></p> <ul> <li><code>in_app_browser = false</code> disables the built-in browser pane.</li> <li><code>in_app_updates = false</code> disables the ChatGPT desktop app's own updater on restart, where supported. It doesn't affect external package deployment or extend support for older app versions. For setup and rollout guidance, see <a href=\"#/article/a-24c62848a3326493\">Manage app updates</a>.</li> <li><code>browser_use = false</code> disables supported Browser Use capabilities. External Browser Use and native-app Computer Use have separate controls.</li> <li><code>browser_use_full_cdp_access = false</code> disables full CDP access in the local runtime, including Browser Developer mode, and prevents the ChatGPT desktop app from enabling the corresponding setting.</li> <li><code>browser_use_external = false</code> disables agent-driven Browser Use through supported browser extensions.</li> <li><code>computer_use = false</code> disables native-app Computer Use, including operating a browser as a native app, along with Record &amp; Replay and related install or setup flows.</li> </ul> <p><strong>Disabling Browser Use alone does not block every browser path.</strong> Native-app Computer Use can still operate a browser when that capability and app access are allowed; Browser Use site and capability rules do not apply to that path. To block it, set <strong>Computer use</strong> to <strong>Force off</strong> (<code>computer_use = false</code>), or <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-native-app-access\">deny the browser app in App rules</a> if other native apps should remain available. Review <strong>External browser use</strong> and <strong>In-app browser</strong> separately. See <a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801?anchor=browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">browser sessions and existing sign-ins</a> for the controls that apply to each path.</p> <p>If you omit these keys, policy allows the features, subject to normal client, platform, and rollout availability.</p> <h4 id=\"restrict-locked-computer-use\">Restrict locked computer use</h4> <p>To prevent users from enabling <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c?anchor=locked-use\">Locked Use</a> on a managed Mac, open <strong>Global</strong> &gt; <strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Desktop app controls</strong>, set <strong>Allow use while locked</strong> to <strong>Disabled</strong>, and select <strong>Save changes</strong>. This setting doesn't turn off Locked Use on a Mac where it's already enabled.</p> <p>The equivalent requirement for the TOML editor or device-managed delivery is:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[computer_use]\nallow_locked_computer_use = false\n</code></pre> <p>This requirement removes the controls for enabling Locked Use. It doesn't turn off Locked Use if it's already enabled. If you omit it, normal product availability and the user's local setting still apply.</p> <h4 id=\"disable-appshots\">Disable Appshots</h4> <p>Appshots let supported clients include application snapshots. To disable them for cloud-managed users, open <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security and set the top-level <code>allow_appshots</code> field in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. For device-managed deployments, use the applicable local requirements source:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_appshots = false\n</code></pre> <p>Where Appshots are available, <code>allow_appshots = false</code> disables them. If you omit the key, requirements don't constrain Appshots, and normal product availability checks apply. App-server clients that read effective requirements through <code>configRequirements/read</code> receive the same restriction as <code>allowAppshots</code>; an omitted or <code>null</code> <code>allowAppshots</code> value doesn't disable Appshots.</p> <h4 id=\"disable-device-remote-control\">Disable device remote control</h4> <p>To disable <a href=\"#/article/a-171fdaa74f75310f?anchor=pick-up-work-from-another-device\">device remote control</a> for cloud-managed users, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security and set the top-level <code>allow_remote_control</code> field in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. You can also set <code>allow_remote_control</code> in a supported local requirements source:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_remote_control = false\n</code></pre> <p>Where device remote control is supported, <code>allow_remote_control = false</code> disables it. If you omit the key, requirements don't constrain device remote control, and normal product availability checks apply. This requirement doesn't disable SSH remote connections.</p> <h2 id=\"set-approvals-and-execution-permissions\">Set approvals and execution permissions</h2> <p>Use this section to set approval behavior, permission boundaries, and starting defaults. Begin with <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\">Policy guardrails</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">Defaults</a>. The later tasks explain TOML requirements for reviewer enforcement, custom permission profiles, filesystem restrictions, host overrides, and command rules.</p> <h3 id=\"admin-enforced-requirements-requirementstoml\">Admin-enforced requirements (requirements.toml)</h3> <p>Requirements constrain security-sensitive settings (approval policy, approvals reviewer, automatic review policy, sandbox mode, permission profiles, web search mode, managed hooks, which MCP servers users can enable, and which plugin marketplace sources they can use). When resolving configuration (for example from <code>config.toml</code>, <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=profiles\">profile files</a>, or CLI config overrides), if a value conflicts with an enforced rule, the local client falls back to a compatible value and notifies the user. If you configure an <code>mcp_servers</code> allowlist, the client enables an MCP server only when both its name and identity match an approved entry; otherwise, the client disables it.</p> <p>Requirements can also constrain <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=feature-flags\">feature flags</a> via the <code>[features]</code> table in <code>requirements.toml</code>. Note that features aren't always security-sensitive, but enterprises can pin values if desired. Omitted keys remain unconstrained.</p> <p>For Codex 0.138.0 or later, prefer <a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">permission profiles</a> with <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> and managed <code>default_permissions</code>. Use <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code> only for legacy deployments that still configure <code>sandbox_mode</code>.</p> <p>For the exact key list, see the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> section in Configuration Reference</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"set-approval-sandbox-and-web-search-guardrails\">Set approval, sandbox, and web search guardrails</h3> <p>Use <strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Policy guardrails</strong> to limit supported client choices. These controls constrain configuration; they don't select the starting value. Set the starting value separately under <strong>Defaults</strong>.</p> <ol> <li>In <strong>Global</strong>, open <strong>Approval policies</strong> and select the allowed approval styles. For example, <strong>Ask when needed</strong> allows the agent to request approval for actions such as sandbox escalation. <strong>Never ask</strong> prevents approval requests; it doesn't grant unrestricted execution.</li> <li>Open <strong>Sandbox modes</strong> to limit legacy sandbox choices. <strong>Read-only</strong> restricts writes, <strong>Workspace write</strong> permits writes within the workspace and configured writable roots, and <strong>Full access</strong> removes filesystem sandboxing. Use <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">permission-profile requirements</a> for deployments that use permission profiles instead of <code>sandbox_mode</code>.</li> <li>In <strong>Global</strong>, open <strong>Web search modes</strong> to limit web search. Choose <strong>Cached</strong> to permit cached search without live search, or <strong>Disabled</strong> to turn off web search. Web search restrictions are separate from command network rules.</li> <li>Review the allowed choices and corresponding defaults, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>.</li> </ol> <p>Leaving a requirement unset imposes no limit under that policy. Other applicable policies still apply. The <strong>Ask for untrusted commands</strong> option corresponds to <code>untrusted</code> in the requirements allowlist; it doesn't restore the retired explicit <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code> setting. Follow the migration guidance below for that case.</p> <p>To constrain web search, use <strong>Global</strong> &gt; <strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Policy guardrails</strong> &gt; <strong>Web search modes</strong>. The equivalent TOML is:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\"] # \"disabled\" remains implicitly allowed\n</code></pre> <p><code>allowed_web_search_modes = []</code> allows only <code>\"disabled\"</code>. For example, <code>allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\"]</code> prevents live web search even in <code>danger-full-access</code> sessions.</p> <h3 id=\"set-configuration-defaults\">Set configuration defaults</h3> <p>Defaults give members a starting configuration. They don't enforce a restriction or grant access to a workspace capability.</p> <ol> <li>Open the policy's <strong>Defaults</strong> card. Keep agent-wide defaults in <strong>Global</strong>; use an available environment tab for supported execution defaults.</li> <li>Under <strong>Command execution</strong>, choose the starting <strong>Approval policy</strong> and <strong>Approval reviewer</strong>, and the applicable <strong>Sandbox mode</strong> or <strong>Default permissions</strong>. The reviewer determines who reviews approval requests by default; the approval policy determines when requests can occur.</li> <li>Under <strong>Web search</strong>, choose the starting <strong>Web search</strong> mode and <strong>Web search context</strong>. Under <strong>Apps and tools</strong>, review <strong>Apps/connectors</strong>, <strong>Open-world tools</strong>, and <strong>Destructive tools</strong> for supported app tools.</li> <li>Confirm the defaults meet your <strong>Requirements</strong>, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>.</li> </ol> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> for supported defaults without visual controls, such as plugin marketplace configuration. These cloud defaults use <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">normal configuration precedence</a>. They differ from the <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=precedence-and-layering\">legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code> precedence</a>, which places managed defaults above local configuration at startup.</p> <h3 id=\"example-requirementstoml\">Example requirements.toml</h3> <p>This example blocks <code>--ask-for-approval never</code> and <code>--sandbox danger-full-access</code> (including <code>--yolo</code>):</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"untrusted\", \"on-request\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Here, <code>untrusted</code> preserves the stricter approval behavior derived from <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code>; it does not make <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code> a supported explicit setting.</p> <h3 id=\"migrate-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">Migrate the retired <code>untrusted</code> approval policy</h3> <p>Codex and ChatGPT Work no longer support <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code>. Remove it from managed defaults, legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code>, and any user, project, profile, or startup configuration that sets it.</p> <p>For interactive, read-only use, select <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code> with a read-only sandbox or permission profile allowed by your managed requirements. Commands allowed by that sandbox can run without approval.</p> <p>To keep stricter command approvals, omit an explicit <code>approval_policy</code>, set <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code> in the project's entry in user-level <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, and keep <code>untrusted</code> in <code>allowed_approval_policies</code>. This also disables project-local configuration. Setting <code>on-request</code> explicitly overrides that policy. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">Migrate from the retired <code>untrusted</code> approval policy</a> for examples and security tradeoffs.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-automatic-review-policy\">Configure automatic review policy</h3> <p>To make automatic review the default, use <strong>Global</strong> &gt; <strong>Defaults</strong> &gt; <strong>Command execution</strong> &gt; <strong>Approval reviewer</strong>. A default lets users choose another permitted reviewer. To require automatic review or supply your organization's review instructions, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> and edit Global <code>requirements.toml</code> instead. These requirements are separate from the browser's automatic-review controls.</p> <p>Use <code>allowed_approvals_reviewers</code> to require or allow automatic review. Set it to <code>[\"auto_review\"]</code> to require automatic review, or include <code>\"user\"</code> when users can choose manual approval.</p> <p>Set <code>guardian_policy_config</code> to replace the tenant-specific section of the automatic review policy. The local runtime still uses the built-in reviewer template and output contract. Managed <code>guardian_policy_config</code> takes precedence over local <code>[auto_review].policy</code>.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"auto_review\"]\n\nguardian_policy_config = \"\"\"\n## Environment Profile\n- Trusted internal destinations include github.com/my-org, artifacts.example.com,\n  and internal CI systems.\n\n## Tenant Risk Taxonomy and Allow/Deny Rules\n- Treat uploads to unapproved third-party file-sharing services as high risk.\n- Deny actions that expose credentials or private source code to untrusted\n  destinations.\n\"\"\"\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"control-available-permission-profiles\">Control available permission profiles</h3> <p>Use <strong>Defaults</strong> &gt; <strong>Command execution</strong> &gt; <strong>Default permissions</strong> to choose the built-in profile users start with. To enforce which profiles users can select or to define custom profiles, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> and configure <code>requirements.toml</code> for the applicable Global or supported execution environment scope. Choosing a default alone doesn't restrict the available profiles.</p> <p>Use <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> to control which built-in and custom <a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">permission profiles</a> users can select. This is the permission-profile counterpart to <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code>; use the allowlist that matches how your users select permissions.</p> <p>Permission-profile allowlists require Codex 0.138.0 or later. Codex 0.137.0 and earlier ignore <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> and managed <code>default_permissions</code>.</p> <p>Use the permission-profile examples below only after every managed client runs a supporting release. Don't deploy managed custom profiles until the fleet upgrade is complete.</p> <p>When present, the table is the complete list of allowed profiles. It allows profiles set to <code>true</code> and denies profiles omitted or set to <code>false</code>, including built-ins added in future Codex versions.</p> <h4 id=\"allow-the-standard-profiles\">Allow the standard profiles</h4> <p>This policy allows read-only and workspace access, but not full access:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n# \":danger-full-access\" is omitted, so it is denied.\n</code></pre> <h4 id=\"add-a-managed-least-privilege-default\">Add a managed least-privilege default</h4> <p>Admins can define a custom profile in the same requirements source. Use organization-specific profile names that won't collide with names in users' loaded config. Custom names can't start with <code>:</code> or use the reserved <code>filesystem</code> name.</p> <p>Don't deploy managed custom profiles to clients running Codex 0.137.0 or earlier. Those clients recognize the profile table but not the managed default that selects it.</p> <p>For example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"acme_review_only\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\nacme_review_only = true\n# \":danger-full-access\" is intentionally omitted, so it is denied.\n\n[permissions.acme_review_only]\ndescription = \"Review code without modifying the workspace.\"\nextends = \":read-only\"\n</code></pre> <h4 id=\"allow-only-enterprise-defined-profiles\">Allow only enterprise-defined profiles</h4> <p>Omit all built-ins when users should select only admin-defined profiles:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"acme_workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\nacme_workspace = true\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace]\ndescription = \"Workspace access with sensitive files denied.\"\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace.filesystem]\nglob_scan_max_depth = 3\n\n[permissions.acme_workspace.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>The custom profile can extend <code>:workspace</code> even though users can't select the built-in <code>:workspace</code> profile directly.</p> <h4 id=\"turn-off-a-profile-allowed-by-another-source\">Turn off a profile allowed by another source</h4> <p>Permission allowlists combine by profile name. Because cloud requirements have higher precedence than system requirements, cloud requirements can use <code>false</code> to turn off a profile allowed by the system file.</p> <p>Cloud requirements:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \":read-only\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = false\n</code></pre> <p>System requirements:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true  # Not honored because cloud requirements set this to false.\n</code></pre> <p>Set <code>default_permissions</code> explicitly to an allowed profile. If it's omitted, the local runtime defaults to <code>:workspace</code> only when both <code>:workspace</code> and <code>:read-only</code> are explicitly allowed. When <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> is absent, managed requirements don't restrict which profile names users can select. Every entry must name a built-in profile or a custom profile defined in a loaded config or requirements source. Define custom profiles in managed requirements to control their behavior centrally.</p> <h3 id=\"enforce-deny-read-requirements\">Enforce deny-read requirements</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to add filesystem restrictions to the applicable Global or supported execution environment <code>requirements.toml</code>. These restrictions block reads of sensitive paths; a read-only default by itself doesn't hide files.</p> <p>Admins can deny reads for exact paths or glob patterns with <code>[permissions.filesystem]</code>. Users can't weaken these requirements with local configuration.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\n  # values can be absolute paths...\n  \"/**/*.env\",\n  # ...or relative to $HOME/%USERPROFILE% using `~`.\n  \"~/.ssh\",\n  # But relative paths starting with `./` are not allowed.\n]\n</code></pre> <p>When deny-read requirements are present, the local runtime rejects full-access permissions and keeps local execution in a read-only or workspace sandbox so it can enforce them. On native Windows, managed <code>deny_read</code> applies to direct file tools; shell subprocess reads don't use this sandbox rule.</p> <h3 id=\"override-sandbox-requirements-by-host\">Override sandbox requirements by host</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security to author host-specific requirements. Environment controls select an execution environment; host patterns select matching machines within the supported runtime.</p> <p>Use <code>[[remote_sandbox_config]]</code> when one managed policy should apply different sandbox requirements on different hosts. For example, you can keep a stricter default for laptops while allowing workspace writes on matching dev boxes or CI runners. Host-specific entries currently override <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code> only:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\"]\n\n[[remote_sandbox_config]]\nhostname_patterns = [\"*.devbox.example.com\", \"runner-??.ci.example.com\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <p>The local runtime compares each <code>hostname_patterns</code> entry against the best-effort resolved host name. It prefers the fully qualified domain name when available and falls back to the local host name. Matching is case-insensitive; <code>*</code> matches any sequence of characters, and <code>?</code> matches one character.</p> <p>The first matching <code>[[remote_sandbox_config]]</code> entry wins within the same requirements source. If no entry matches, the local runtime keeps the top-level <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code>. Host name matching is for policy selection only; don't treat it as authenticated device proof.</p> <h3 id=\"enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\">Enforce command rules from requirements</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security to add command rules and MCP server restrictions to Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. Command rules specify approval or denial decisions for matching commands; MCP restrictions limit which server identities the client can enable. The <strong>Approval policies</strong> control doesn't author either of these rule sets.</p> <p>Admins can also enforce restrictive command rules from <code>requirements.toml</code> using a <code>[rules]</code> table. These rules merge with regular <code>.rules</code> files, and the most restrictive decision still wins.</p> <p>Unlike <code>.rules</code>, requirements rules must specify <code>decision</code>, and that decision must be <code>\"prompt\"</code> or <code>\"forbidden\"</code> (not <code>\"allow\"</code>).</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"rm\" }], decision = \"forbidden\", justification = \"Use git clean -fd instead.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before mutating history.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>To restrict which MCP servers a local client can enable, add an <code>mcp_servers</code> approved list. For stdio servers, match on <code>command</code>; for streamable HTTP servers, match on <code>url</code>:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.docs]\nidentity = { command = \"codex-mcp\" }\n\n[mcp_servers.remote]\nidentity = { url = \"https://example.com/mcp\" }\n</code></pre> <p>The string form of <code>identity.command</code> matches only the configured <code>command</code>. It doesn't inspect <code>args</code>, <code>cwd</code>, <code>env</code>, or <code>env_vars</code>.</p> <p>To constrain a complete stdio invocation, match the executable and each positional argument:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal.identity]\ncommand = { executable = \"/usr/local/bin/codex-mcp\", args = [\n  { match = \"exact\", value = \"serve\" },\n  { match = \"prefix\", value = \"--workspace=\" },\n] }\n</code></pre> <p>The executable, argument count, and argument order must match. Argument and URL rules support <code>exact</code>, <code>prefix</code>, and full-value <code>regex</code> matching. Structured command rules still don't inspect <code>cwd</code>, <code>env</code>, or <code>env_vars</code>. Plugin-bundled MCP servers use the same identity shapes under <code>plugins.&lt;plugin&gt;.mcp_servers.&lt;server&gt;</code>.</p> <p>If <code>mcp_servers</code> is present but empty, the local client disables all MCP servers.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-network-access\">Configure network access</h2> <p>Command networking and desktop-app networking use different controls. Start with <strong>Network use controls</strong> for supported command traffic, review the environment and runtime limits, then use the separate desktop-app requirements if needed. Browser site rules remain in <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">Control browser and Computer Use</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-network-access-requirements\">Configure network access requirements</h3> <p><code>[experimental_network]</code> is experimental and may change. Do not enable these requirements broadly across an enterprise deployment without validating them on the local client versions and operating systems your users run. Windows support is still limited; avoid applying this policy to Windows users unless you have tested it in your environment.</p> <p>Use <strong>Requirements</strong> &gt; <strong>Network use controls</strong> to manage supported command networking in Agent security. Configure a shared baseline in <strong>Global</strong>, or an available <strong>Local Environments</strong> or <strong>Codex Cloud Environments</strong> tab for environment-specific execution settings.</p> <ol> <li>Set <strong>Managed networking</strong> to <strong>On</strong> to apply domain rules to managed command traffic. Adding domain rules alone doesn't activate the managed proxy.</li> <li>Under <strong>Network rules</strong>, select <strong>Add network rule</strong>. Enter a <strong>Domain</strong> such as <code>api.openai.com</code> or <code>**.example.com</code>, without a URL scheme or path. Choose <strong>Allow</strong> or <strong>Deny</strong> under <strong>Access</strong>, then select <strong>Save rule</strong> to update the draft. Repeat for other destinations.</li> <li>Set <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> to <strong>On</strong> if user settings and per-domain approvals must not expand the managed allowlist. Include the required Allow entries; a deny-only list doesn't allow other destinations.</li> <li>Review <strong>Allow local network access</strong> for workflows that need local listeners or private destinations. Direct local connections may bypass the proxy's domain rules. Review the Codex Cloud connectivity limits below before changing this setting.</li> <li>Review inherited rules and validation messages, then select <strong>Save changes</strong>. Test a request that should succeed and one that should be blocked in every affected execution environment.</li> </ol> <p>Setting <strong>Managed networking</strong> to <strong>Off</strong> clears that scope's authored network rules and stores an explicit off value; it doesn't disable internet access. <strong>Not set</strong> removes that scope's authored network settings so applicable policy or runtime defaults can apply. Removing environment settings can reveal inherited Global rules. Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> for advanced network fields or existing legacy domain lists that the visual editor can't represent.</p> <p>Use <code>[experimental_network]</code> in <code>requirements.toml</code> when administrators should define network access requirements centrally. These requirements are separate from the user <code>features.network_proxy</code> toggle: they can configure sandbox networking without that feature flag, but they don't grant command network access when the active sandbox keeps networking off. Set <code>experimental_network.enabled = true</code> to activate the managed proxy; domain rules alone do not make the proxy active.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"**.example.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"blocked.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n\"**.exfil.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>Use <code>experimental_network.managed_allowed_domains_only = true</code> only when you also define administrator-owned <code>\"allow\"</code> entries in <code>[experimental_network.domains]</code> and want those rules to be exclusive. If it's <code>true</code> without managed allow rules, user-added domain allow rules don't remain effective. Do not combine the canonical <code>domains</code> map with the legacy <code>allowed_domains</code> or <code>denied_domains</code> lists.</p> <p><code>*.example.com</code> matches subdomains only. <code>**.example.com</code> matches the apex domain and its subdomains. A matching deny rule wins over an allow rule.</p> <p>The domain syntax, local/private destination rules, deny-over-allow behavior, and DNS rebinding limitations are the same as the sandbox networking behavior described in <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=network-isolation\">Agent approvals &amp; security</a>.</p> <p>The proxy routes local commands that run inside the sandbox. Browser tools also check managed network denies and exclusive allowlists before accessing an origin. This is a separate policy check, not routing browser traffic through the command proxy. It doesn't filter web search, apps and connectors, MCP servers, native-app traffic, Codex service requests, or other capability-specific traffic. Use the controls for each surface:</p> <ul> <li>Use <code>allowed_web_search_modes</code> to restrict web search.</li> <li>Use <code>features.apps = false</code> to disable app and connector integrations, and <code>features.plugins = false</code> to disable plugins where supported.</li> <li>Use the managed <code>mcp_servers</code> approved list to restrict MCP servers.</li> <li>Use feature requirements such as <code>browser_use</code>, <code>in_app_browser</code>, and <code>computer_use</code> to restrict browser and computer-use capabilities.</li> <li>For supported managed Codex Cloud commands, configure Agent Security requirements and the Cloud environment internet settings separately.</li> </ul> <p>A command domain allowlist does not replace these capability-specific controls.</p> <p>On supported managed Codex Cloud execution paths, Agent Security requirements constrain command networking. Codex Cloud environment internet settings apply separately. An allowed domain in Agent Security does not override a restriction in the Cloud environment's internet settings. These command-network controls do not, by themselves, disable hosted web search, apps, or MCP. ChatGPT Work Cloud has separate capability permissions and does not inherit these Agent Security requirements.</p> <p>A managed command allowlist applies to commands using the managed proxy. Where policy permits full sandbox escalation and it is approved, that execution can bypass the command proxy. A narrow network grant is different from full sandbox escalation. Configure enforced approval and sandbox requirements for the intended boundary, and test both ordinary and escalated commands.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>No effective allowed destinations:</strong> When <strong>Managed networking</strong> and <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> are On, ordinary managed commands need effective allowed destinations. If no Allow entries are configured or inherited, those commands have no allowed destinations. A deny-only policy does not implicitly allow the rest of the internet. Add required Allow entries and check inherited rules before saving. This restriction applies to the managed command proxy, not every tool or approved full sandbox escalation.</p> <p><strong>Codex Cloud local/private connectivity:</strong> An explicit Off value for local/private connectivity can prevent Codex Cloud from reaching its upstream proxy, even when the destination domain is allowed. Check the final <code>allow_local_binding</code> value and identify which policy or setting supplies it. On the supported Cloud proxy path, this defaults to true only when no applicable requirement, selected network profile, or proxy feature setting supplies a value. An inherited false still counts as an explicit setting. Where supported, set a higher-priority Cloud override to change this value for Codex Cloud without changing the Global value used by Local. This does not add domain Allow entries. Verify executor support before relying on the override. Do not apply this Cloud default to Local.</p> </blockquote> <p>When environment requirements are empty, Global requirements apply. <strong>Managed networking</strong> set to <strong>Off</strong> is separate from the Cloud environment's Internet access setting. See <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=execution-controls-executor\">Agent Security execution controls</a> for details about execution scope.</p> <h3 id=\"network-policy-precedence-and-runtime-limits\">Network policy precedence and runtime limits</h3> <p>Within a given policy, the order from highest to lowest is OS-specific environment override → all-OS environment override → Global. A higher-priority policy still wins over a lower-priority policy, even when the lower-priority policy is more specific. Some requirements have field-specific merge rules. The runtime then enforces the resolved requirements. Consider the field-specific network cases below separately from policy priority.</p> <p>These examples compare environment and Global settings within the same policy, with managed networking already configured and no higher-priority policy changing the values. They describe field-specific merge and runtime behavior.</p> <h4 id=\"set-different-domain-access-by-environment\">Set different domain access by environment</h4> <p>For the same domain rule within a policy, an admin environment override can allow a domain denied in Global or deny a domain allowed in Global. Without an environment override, the Global rule is inherited. Other effective Deny rules or access controls can still block a request.</p> <p><strong>How domain rules combine</strong></p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Global rule</th> <th>Environment rule</th> <th>Domain-policy result</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Deny</td> <td>Allow</td> <td>Allowed by the environment override, subject to the conditions below.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow</td> <td>Deny</td> <td>Blocked in that environment.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow</td> <td>Allow</td> <td>Allowed by these domain rules.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Deny</td> <td>Deny</td> <td>Blocked by these domain rules.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allow or Deny</td> <td>No override</td> <td>Inherits the Global rule.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>These outcomes compare the same domain key within one policy, with no higher-priority policy changing the result. A higher-priority value replaces the same key. Other inherited keys remain. An Allow does not bypass a different matching Deny, such as an inherited wildcard. An empty environment map does not clear inherited rules.</p> <p><strong>Example:</strong> Deny <code>packages.example.com</code> in Global and allow it in Development. Development can permit it under the conditions above. Production inherits the Global Deny unless overridden.</p> <p>Confirm the deployed executor supports these merge rules. Coverage for regular managed executors still needs validation.</p> <p>Orchestrator limits. Managed HTTP/SOCKS listener ports and non-loopback proxy listeners are unsupported by the cloud runtime; socket-rule support depends on the execution path. A local-execution setting in the table below is not a supported Orchestrator configuration.</p> <h4 id=\"other-network-runtime-limits\">Other network runtime limits</h4> <p>All field names below are under <code>experimental_network</code> in <code>requirements.toml</code>. The arrows show a Global value followed by an environment value within the same policy. These local-execution cases do not establish Orchestrator support for the same settings.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th><strong>Setting and attempted override</strong></th> <th><strong>Runtime behavior</strong></th> <th><strong>Admin action</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>domains: deny → allow</td> <td>The environment Allow replaces the same Global domain key on supported paths. Other matching Deny rules still apply.</td> <td>Test both allowed and blocked requests on the deployed executor.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>managed_allowed_domains_only</code>: true → false</td> <td>Do not infer exclusivity behavior from domain-key composition. With effective <code>enabled = true</code> and <code>managed_allowed_domains_only = true</code>, ordinary commands need effective Allow entries.</td> <td>Review effective settings and inherited Allow entries. A deny-only policy does not allow the rest of the internet.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>allow_local_binding</code>: false → true</td> <td>On the supported Codex Cloud proxy path, an explicit false can prevent upstream-proxy access. A supported higher-priority Cloud override can change it.</td> <td>Check the source of the effective value and executor support. Do not apply the Cloud default to Local.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Proxy activation, exclusivity, upstream-proxy behavior, and Unix-socket rules have executor-specific behavior. Do not generalize domain-key composition to these settings. Verify the effective values and test the deployed runtime. For Codex Cloud local/private connectivity, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">Configure network access requirements</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"verify-behavior-before-rollout\">Verify behavior before rollout</h4> <p>Test the actual destinations, local listeners, proxy behavior, and socket paths your workflows use. Check an action that should succeed and one that should be blocked in each affected environment. Check that the deployed runtime version supports each setting you use.</p> <h3 id=\"control-desktop-app-network-destinations\">Control desktop app network destinations</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security to configure these desktop-specific requirements in Global. The <strong>Network use controls</strong> UI manages command networking, which is a separate policy.</p> <p>Use <code>[application.network]</code> in <code>requirements.toml</code> to restrict the desktop app's network destinations. With <code>enabled = true</code>, external requests must use HTTPS or WSS and match an exact domain with an <code>\"allow\"</code> value in <code>[application.network.domains]</code>. Subdomains aren't implicitly allowed. An empty domain map permits no external destinations. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a> for the supported keys.</p> <p>This policy is separate from command networking and browser origin rules. It doesn't impose destination restrictions on native modules or spawned processes, and it doesn't govern Work Cloud execution.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-plugins-and-managed-hooks\">Configure plugins and managed hooks</h2> <p>For these tasks, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security or a supported local configuration source. Marketplace defaults, enforced plugin restrictions, and managed hooks have different purposes; choose the task that matches the policy you need.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-plugin-marketplaces-and-defaults\">Configure plugin marketplaces and defaults</h3> <p>Use <strong>Agent security</strong> &gt; <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to define local or Git marketplaces and plugin defaults in the Global <code>config.toml</code> document. You can also distribute these settings in system <code>config.toml</code>. They provide starting marketplace and plugin configuration, not enforced policy. To limit which sources members can use, configure <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-plugin-marketplace-sources\">marketplace source requirements</a>.</p> <p>See <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a> for configuration keys, <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">Configuration precedence</a> for overrides, and <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">repo plugin settings</a> for project-level configuration. <a href=\"#/article/a-ab115ea47731f92a\">Workspace GitHub import and sync</a> is separate.</p> <h3 id=\"control-plugin-availability\">Control plugin availability</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security for enforced plugin feature settings in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>, or distribute them through local requirements for device-managed clients. <strong>Defaults</strong> &gt; <strong>Apps and tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Apps/connectors</strong> sets app-tool defaults; it isn't the enforced <code>features.plugins</code> switch. For workspace plugin permissions, see <a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">Apps and connectors</a>.</p> <p>To turn off plugins in supported local clients, set <code>features.plugins</code> to <code>false</code> in <code>requirements.toml</code>:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">features.plugins = false\n</code></pre> <p>This setting also applies when users sign in to Codex with an API key. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>features.plugins</code> reference</a> for the supported configuration.</p> <h3 id=\"restrict-plugin-marketplace-sources\">Restrict plugin marketplace sources</h3> <p>Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to configure marketplace source requirements in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>, or deliver them through local requirements. These rules limit which local or Git sources users can add and use. They are separate from the <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-plugin-marketplaces-and-defaults\">marketplace defaults</a>.</p> <p>To restrict plugin marketplace sources, set <code>restrict_to_allowed_sources = true</code> and define one or more source rules:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[marketplaces]\nrestrict_to_allowed_sources = true\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.company_plugins]\nsource = \"git\"\nurl = \"https://github.com/example/company-plugins.git\"\nref = \"main\"\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.internal_git]\nsource = \"host_pattern\"\nhost_pattern = '^git\\.example\\.com$'\n\n[marketplaces.allowed_sources.local_plugins]\nsource = \"local\"\npath = \"/opt/company/codex-plugins\"\n</code></pre> <p>Git rules match the normalized repository URL and, when present, an exact <code>ref</code>. Host patterns are regular expressions matched against the lowercase Git host; use <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> for a whole-host match. Local rules require an absolute, normalized path. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> reference</a> for the full schema and merge behavior.</p> <p>These requirements reject unmatched marketplace add, plugin install, and configured Git marketplace refresh operations. They also filter configured marketplaces and their plugins at runtime.</p> <p>The OpenAI-curated Git marketplaces, including the API-key catalog, must also match the source allowlist. To allow them, include the following Git source without a <code>ref</code> constraint:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[marketplaces.allowed_sources.openai_curated]\nsource = \"git\"\nurl = \"https://github.com/openai/plugins.git\"\n</code></pre> <p>To exclude the curated catalogs, omit that source and ensure no broader host rule allows it. Bundled plugins and remotely installed workspace plugins are separate from this curated Git source policy.</p> <p>These source restrictions apply only where a local client supports plugin marketplace operations: ChatGPT and Codex in the desktop app, and Codex CLI. They don't control plugin use in ChatGPT on the web or mobile, and they don't add plugins to the IDE extension.</p> <h3 id=\"enforce-managed-hooks-from-requirements\">Enforce managed hooks from requirements</h3> <p>Managed hooks run administrator-defined actions at supported task or tool events. Use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> in Agent security to configure them in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>; the settings controls don't include a dedicated hook editor. The execution paths below determine which hook types can run.</p> <p>When managed policy and remote hooks are enabled, Work Cloud with local access and dots use admin-managed remote MCP hooks on the cloud orchestrator. Configure <code>mcp_tool</code> handlers in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. Work Cloud without local access and personal accounts do not use these enterprise hooks. Command/shell, prompt, and agent handlers; hooks from local configuration, plugins, or local directories; environment-scoped hooks; and <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP hooks are not supported with cloud orchestration, even when tools execute locally. When both orchestration and execution are local, existing supported hooks continue to work in local-only Work and Codex threads. Admins can still configure supported managed hooks in Agent Security for those workflows.</p> <p>Before relying on these hooks, test callback connectivity, required events, and failure behavior. An explicit supported denial can block an action, but a <code>PreToolUse</code> callback error, timeout, or malformed response can fail the hook without blocking the tool. MCP hooks do not provide a complete Compliance API audit trail. The following script and directory examples retain their Codex scope.</p> <p>Admins can also define managed lifecycle hooks directly in <code>requirements.toml</code>. Use <code>[hooks]</code> for the hook configuration itself, and point <code>managed_dir</code> at the directory where your MDM or endpoint-management tooling installs the referenced scripts.</p> <p>To enforce managed hooks even for users who turned hooks off locally, pin <code>[features].hooks = true</code> alongside <code>[hooks]</code>. To skip user, project, session, and plugin hooks while still allowing managed hooks, set <code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code>.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_managed_hooks_only = true\n\n[features]\nhooks = true\n\n[hooks]\nmanaged_dir = \"/enterprise/hooks\"\nwindows_managed_dir = 'C:\\enterprise\\hooks'\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\nmatcher = \"^Bash$\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\ntype = \"command\"\ncommand = \"python3 /enterprise/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"\ncommand_windows = 'py -3 C:\\enterprise\\hooks\\pre_tool_use_policy.py'\ntimeout = 30\nstatusMessage = \"Checking managed Bash command\"\n</code></pre> <p>Notes:</p> <ul> <li>The local runtime enforces the hook configuration from <code>requirements.toml</code>, but it doesn't distribute the scripts in <code>managed_dir</code>.</li> <li>Deliver those scripts with your MDM or device-management solution.</li> <li>Managed hook commands should reference absolute script paths under the configured managed directory.</li> <li><code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code> skips hooks from user, project, session, and plugin sources, but still loads hooks from <code>requirements.toml</code> and other managed config layers.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"understand-policy-scope-and-precedence\">Understand policy scope and precedence</h2> <p>Use this section to check where a policy applies, how requirements combine, how clients receive cloud-managed settings, and how to verify the result.</p> <p>For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy governs the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled; applicable local execution requirements govern the connected computer. Work cloud containers retain existing Work Cloud policies, and supported device controls still apply to local execution. Codex keeps its existing configuration behavior. Managed configuration does not grant a workspace seat or feature access. Use <a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">Roles and workspace permissions</a> for those controls.</p> <p><a id=\"agent-security-and-work-sync\"></a></p> <p><span id=\"agent-security-and-local-computer-access\"></span></p> <h3 id=\"agent-security-and-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\">Agent Security and Local computer access with Work Cloud</h3> <p>Use Agent Security in the Admin Console to manage policies and configuration. It replaces Policies &amp; Configuration. Agent Security is available in the Admin Console, independently of Local computer access with Work Cloud. Existing policies, assignments, and ordering are preserved. Review your existing policies in Agent Security. Local computer access with Work Cloud is a separate opt-in. See <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a> for migration guidance.</p> <p>Before moving policy automation to <strong>Agent Security</strong>:</p> <ol> <li> <p>Identify affected integrations. Inventory Terraform configurations and scripts that update policies, and record which policies they manage.</p> </li> <li> <p>Use the policy API to manage Global settings. To manage Local or Codex Cloud settings, use the Agent Security UI. Existing Global API workflows remain available after migration. Test your scripts and Terraform integrations, and confirm that policy assignments and ordering are unchanged.</p> </li> <li> <p>Make any required changes and test the automation. Verify that an intended update reaches the correct Agent Security policy and that the resulting controls are enforced.</p> </li> </ol> <p>Policy migration does not grant local computer access for Work or dots. Reviewing or creating policies is recommended before rollout, but the confirmation flow does not require policy creation to enable access. Before enabling <strong>Allow local computer access</strong>, review the migrated baseline or create a cloud baseline if your policies are currently delivered only through MDM. If <code>enforce_residency</code> is enabled in any cloud policy, <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> is disabled for both Work and dots. This safeguard does not configure workspace residency or, by itself, disable Work Cloud or dots.</p> <p>Environment overrides vary supported settings within a policy. Within a given policy, the order from highest to lowest is OS-specific environment override → all-OS environment override → Global. A higher-priority policy still wins over a lower-priority policy, even when the lower-priority policy is more specific. Some requirements have field-specific merge rules. Confirm which override editors and fields are available for your workspace.</p> <p>When managed policy and remote hooks are enabled, Work Cloud with local access and dots use admin-managed remote MCP hooks on the cloud orchestrator. Configure <code>mcp_tool</code> handlers in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. Work Cloud without local access and personal accounts do not use these enterprise hooks. Command/shell, prompt, and agent handlers; hooks from local configuration, plugins, or local directories; environment-scoped hooks; and <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP hooks are not supported with cloud orchestration, even when tools execute locally. When both orchestration and execution are local, existing supported hooks continue to work in local-only Work and Codex threads. Admins can still configure supported managed hooks in Agent Security for those workflows. Before relying on these hooks, test callback connectivity, required events, and failure behavior. An explicit supported denial can block an action, but a <code>PreToolUse</code> callback error, timeout, or malformed response can fail the hook without blocking the tool. MCP hooks do not provide a complete Compliance API audit trail. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"locations-and-precedence\">Locations and precedence</h3> <p>For local execution, requirements are applied from lower to higher priority as follows. This ordering also applies to local steps in Local computer access with Work Cloud:</p> <ol> <li>System <code>requirements.toml</code> (<code>/etc/codex/requirements.toml</code> on Unix systems, including Linux and macOS, or <code>%ProgramData%\\OpenAI\\Codex\\requirements.toml</code> on Windows).</li> <li>Agent Security requirements delivered in the cloud config bundle.</li> <li>Legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code> fields that the local client reinterprets as requirements.</li> <li>macOS managed preferences (MDM) delivered through <code>com.openai.codex:requirements_toml_base64</code>.</li> </ol> <p>Higher-precedence layers override ordinary scalar and list values from lower layers. Tables merge by key, while requirements such as rules, hooks, and filesystem restrictions have field-specific composition behavior. Use the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> reference</a> for the current schema instead of assuming that every field merges the same way.</p> <p>For backward compatibility, supported local clients reinterpret the legacy <code>approval_policy</code>, <code>approvals_reviewer</code>, and <code>sandbox_mode</code> fields as requirements. This conversion adds compatibility choices where necessary; use <code>requirements.toml</code> for explicit allowlists.</p> <h3 id=\"precedence-for-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\">Precedence for Local computer access with Work Cloud</h3> <p>For Work with local access and dots, distinguish Global orchestrator policy from execution policy. Applicable local requirements govern the connected computer. Work cloud containers and dots cloud computers use their own execution configuration and requirements, rather than the managed environment bundle used by other executors.</p> <p>For local execution, MDM and legacy managed-device requirements rank above Agent Security. The device's system requirements file ranks below Agent Security. Within each policy, resolve OS-specific environment overrides before all-OS environment overrides, then Global. Policy priority wins over specificity across policies.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Control</th> <th>Local Work without sync and local Codex</th> <th>Local computer access with Work Cloud</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Enterprise requirements</td> <td>Follow the local requirements order above.</td> <td>Apply to local executors. Work cloud containers retain existing Work Cloud policies.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Device execution restrictions</td> <td>Enforced by supported local controls.</td> <td>Follow the local requirements order above, including MDM and legacy requirements above Agent Security.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Environment overrides</td> <td>Use the configuration model for the relevant product.</td> <td>Within one policy: OS-specific environment override, then all-OS environment override, then Global. A lower-priority policy cannot win through specificity.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Requirements impose constraints, while configuration defaults supply starting values. For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy applies through the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled. Applicable local <code>requirements.toml</code> requirements govern execution on a connected computer. Work cloud containers and dots cloud computers use their own execution configuration and requirements, rather than the managed environment bundle used by other executor types. Local execution restrictions do not automatically apply to these cloud computers. Review cloud capability permissions and test local and cloud execution separately.</p> <p>Keep orchestrator controls, including approvals and web search, in Global. Use the dedicated Allowed approval policies and Allowed web search modes controls where available, and TOML for other supported fields. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a> for the field list and execution scope.</p> <h3 id=\"cloud-managed-requirements\">Cloud-managed requirements</h3> <p>When a user signs in with ChatGPT on a supported plan, supported local clients can receive admin-enforced requirements associated with the workspace. This is a delivery channel for <code>requirements.toml</code>-compatible policy. It doesn't grant workspace access or replace workspace RBAC. Authentication requirements must be <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=manage-authentication-locally\">managed locally</a>.</p> <p>Configure common requirements with the <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-a-policy-in-agent-security\">Agent security controls</a>. For a policy that allows only on-request approvals and read-only or workspace-write sandboxes, use <strong>Approval policies</strong> and <strong>Sandbox modes</strong> under <strong>Policy guardrails</strong>. To also prompt before a supported shell entry point runs, use <strong>Edit as TOML</strong> to add the <code>[rules]</code> table to Global <code>requirements.toml</code>.</p> <p>The complete TOML equivalent is:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ any_of = [\"bash\", \"sh\", \"zsh\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require explicit approval for shell entry points\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>Check that every managed client version supports the keys you select. Assign the policy to the intended users or groups and verify that it takes effect on their supported clients. Use the configuration reference for the current schema and the administration surface for current assignment behavior.</p> <p>The service selects the enterprise-managed requirement layers that apply to the signed-in identity. The local client evaluates those layers with the other requirements sources described in <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">Locations and precedence</a>. Use the current administration surface for workspace-side creation and assignment. Don't rely on a copied group-matching algorithm; the administration service owns that behavior and can change it independently of the local requirements format.</p> <p>For supported keys and examples, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=example-requirementstoml\">Example requirements.toml</a> and the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code> reference</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"how-local-clients-apply-cloud-managed-requirements\">How local clients apply cloud-managed requirements</h4> <p>The local loading and cache behavior below describes supported local clients. It does not establish when a changed cloud policy takes effect in an already-running task using Local computer access with Work Cloud. Verify the effective policy before relying on a changed restriction.</p> <p>When a user starts a supported local client and signs in with ChatGPT on a supported plan, the client first checks for a valid, identity-matched cache entry. If no valid entry is available, the client fetches the applicable bundle with retries and writes a signed cache entry on success. If the request fails or times out and no valid cache is available, the cloud config bundle load returns an error rather than silently starting without the cloud-managed requirements layer.</p> <p>After cache resolution, the client composes the cloud requirements with the other requirements layers described above. A background refresh updates the cached bundle and makes it available to subsequent configuration loads. This doesn't guarantee that an already-running task immediately uses a changed policy. Some desktop settings require an app restart; follow any restart prompt and verify the effective settings before relying on the change.</p> <h3 id=\"confirm-the-admin-and-employee-experience\">Confirm the admin and employee experience</h3> <p>Assign a person to own each managed policy, record which users or groups should receive it, and document the business reason for any filesystem, network, approval, or permission-profile restriction.</p> <p>Test an allowed workflow and a blocked workflow with a user who has the intended permissions. Check the effective settings in the supported client. A workspace role or group alone does not enforce a local runtime restriction.</p> <p>Use <strong>Test policies</strong> in Agent security to check which policies match a member, group, or role selection. In an editable session, this checks the current draft without saving it. The result shows policy selection, not the final merged configuration or whether a client enforces a restriction. After saving, test the allowed and blocked workflows on each relevant client and execution path.</p> <h2 id=\"deploy-device-managed-configuration\">Deploy device-managed configuration</h2> <p>Use local system requirements or MDM for authentication restrictions and device-managed policies. The legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code> mechanism has different startup precedence from Agent security <strong>Defaults</strong>. The MDM workflow covers both requirements and legacy defaults.</p> <h3 id=\"manage-authentication-locally\">Manage authentication locally</h3> <p>Set <code>allowed_login_methods</code>, <code>allowed_chatgpt_workspaces</code>, <code>cli_auth_credentials_store</code>, and <code>chatgpt_base_url</code> in the local system <code>requirements.toml</code> or macOS MDM requirements. Codex ignores these four fields in cloud-managed requirements. Local authentication requirements apply before credentials load and before Codex retrieves cloud policy.</p> <p>To require ChatGPT login to an approved workspace and store credentials in the OS credential store, use:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]\nallowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"]\ncli_auth_credentials_store = \"keyring\"\n</code></pre> <p><code>allowed_login_methods</code> accepts <code>chatgpt</code>, <code>api</code>, or both. If omitted, this setting doesn't restrict login methods. If set, the list must contain at least one method. <code>api</code> permits API authentication, including Amazon Bedrock. The workspace restriction also applies to <a href=\"#/article/a-f5bc151215cdfb88\">Codex access tokens</a>.</p> <p>User-configured <code>forced_login_method</code> and <code>forced_chatgpt_workspace_id</code> must follow the requirements. When a user selects a workspace, it must also appear in the managed workspace allowlist. If no workspaces match, ChatGPT login is unavailable. API authentication remains available when permitted. If no login method is available, Codex refuses to start.</p> <p>See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">requirements reference</a> for credential storage modes and service URL configuration.</p> <h3 id=\"managed-defaults-managed_configtoml\">Managed defaults (<code>managed_config.toml</code>)</h3> <p>Use <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=set-configuration-defaults\">Agent security Defaults</a> for supported cloud-managed starting values. This section covers the legacy device-managed <code>managed_config.toml</code> mechanism and its different startup precedence. Editing <strong>Defaults</strong> in Agent security doesn't deploy a <code>managed_config.toml</code> file to members' devices.</p> <p>Managed defaults set the configuration a supported local client starts with. At startup, they override the user's local <code>config.toml</code> and any CLI <code>--config</code> overrides. Users can still change those settings during the current run, and the defaults apply again the next time the client starts.</p> <p>GPT-5.5 retires from Codex with ChatGPT sign-in on October 14, 2026, at noon PDT (19:00 UTC). If a managed default, macOS MDM profile, or saved configuration pins <code>gpt-5.5</code> for those users, replace it with an available model. Choose <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> once an administrator has enabled it for the affected users. The OpenAI API and Codex sessions authenticated with an API key aren't affected. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb?anchor=prepare-for-the-gpt-55-retirement\">workspace model availability</a>.</p> <p>For configurations that still pin <code>gpt-5.4</code> or <code>gpt-5.4-mini</code>, follow the <a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb?anchor=prepare-for-the-gpt-54-retirement\">GPT-5.4 migration guidance</a>.</p> <p>Make sure your managed defaults meet your requirements; the local runtime rejects disallowed values.</p> <h4 id=\"precedence-and-layering\">Precedence and layering</h4> <p>The local runtime assembles the effective configuration in this order (top overrides bottom):</p> <ul> <li>Managed preferences (macOS MDM; highest precedence)</li> <li><code>managed_config.toml</code> (system/managed file)</li> <li><code>config.toml</code> (user's base configuration)</li> </ul> <p>CLI <code>--config key=value</code> overrides apply to the base, but managed layers override them. This means each run starts from the managed defaults even if you provide local flags.</p> <p>Cloud <code>config.toml</code> uses <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=configuration-precedence\">normal configuration precedence</a>, not the legacy ordering above. Cloud <code>requirements.toml</code> uses <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">requirements precedence</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"locations\">Locations</h4> <ul> <li>Linux/macOS (Unix): <code>/etc/codex/managed_config.toml</code></li> <li>Windows/non-Unix: <code>~/.codex/managed_config.toml</code></li> </ul> <p>If the file is missing, the local runtime skips the managed layer.</p> <h4 id=\"example-managed_configtoml\">Example managed_config.toml</h4> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Set conservative defaults\napproval_policy = \"on-request\"\nsandbox_mode    = \"workspace-write\"\n\n[sandbox_workspace_write]\nnetwork_access = false             # keep network disabled unless explicitly allowed\n\n[otel]\nenvironment = \"prod\"\nexporter = \"otlp-http\"            # point at your collector\nlog_user_prompt = false            # keep prompts redacted\n# exporter details live under exporter tables; see Monitoring and telemetry above\n</code></pre> <h4 id=\"recommended-guardrails\">Recommended guardrails</h4> <ul> <li>Prefer <code>workspace-write</code> with approvals for most users; reserve full access for controlled containers.</li> <li>Keep <code>network_access = false</code> unless your security review allows a collector or domains required by your workflows.</li> <li>Use managed configuration to pin OTel settings (exporter, environment), but keep <code>log_user_prompt = false</code> unless your policy explicitly allows storing prompt contents.</li> <li>Periodically audit diffs between local <code>config.toml</code> and managed policy to catch drift; managed layers should win over local flags and files.</li> </ul> <h3 id=\"macos-managed-preferences-mdm\">macOS managed preferences (MDM)</h3> <p>On macOS, admins can push a device profile that provides base64-encoded TOML payloads at:</p> <ul> <li>Preference domain: <code>com.openai.codex</code></li> <li>Keys:</li> <li><code>config_toml_base64</code> (managed defaults)</li> <li><code>requirements_toml_base64</code> (requirements)</li> </ul> <p>The local runtime parses these \"managed preferences\" payloads as TOML. For managed defaults (<code>config_toml_base64</code>), managed preferences have the highest precedence. For requirements (<code>requirements_toml_base64</code>), precedence follows the <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=locations-and-precedence\">requirements order</a>. The same requirements-side <code>[features]</code> table works in <code>requirements_toml_base64</code>; use canonical feature keys there as well.</p> <h4 id=\"mdm-setup-workflow\">MDM setup workflow</h4> <p>The local runtime honors standard macOS MDM payloads, so you can distribute settings with tooling like <code>Jamf Pro</code>, <code>Fleet</code>, or <code>Kandji</code>. A lightweight deployment looks like:</p> <ol> <li>Build the managed payload TOML and encode it with <code>base64</code> (no wrapping).</li> <li>Drop the string into your MDM profile under the <code>com.openai.codex</code> domain at <code>config_toml_base64</code> (managed defaults) or <code>requirements_toml_base64</code> (requirements).</li> <li>Push the profile, then ask users to restart the supported local client and confirm the startup config summary reflects the managed values.</li> <li>When revoking or changing policy, update the managed payload; the client reads the refreshed preference the next time it launches.</li> </ol> <p>Avoid embedding secrets or high-churn dynamic values in the payload. Treat the managed TOML like any other MDM setting under change control.</p>"};
